// Generated by authoring/psyc301/build_qa_v4.py from authoring/psyc301/qa-v4/*.py
window.QA_V4 = [{"id": 0, "a": ["**Memory** is the set of processes involved in **retaining, retrieving and using information** about **stimuli, images, events, ideas and skills** after the **original information is no longer present**. The experience itself is over, but something from it is still available to work with.", "- A **past experience can influence current or future behavior** without being visible in the environment. That influence is what shows memory is at work, since nothing in the present surroundings supplies it.", "- Memory is therefore active whenever a past experience affects how someone thinks or behaves now or later, which covers remembering events, ideas and skills alike."]}, {"id": 1, "a": ["**Endel Tulving** described **episodic memory** as a kind of **mental time travel**. When someone remembers a particular event, the present moment is connected back to the experienced past.", "- This links remembered events to the **past in which they were experienced**."]}, {"id": 2, "a": ["**Encoding**, **storage** and **retrieval** are three different operations of memory. **Encoding converts information into a usable form for storage**, so it is the step that gets information in.", "- **Storage retains** the encoded information so that it persists after the original input is gone.", "- **Retrieval makes it available again** when it is needed. Memory depends on all three, and each one can be discussed on its own."]}, {"id": 3, "a": ["Memory is **not an unchanged copy** of an experience. It is an **active system** that can **organize and alter information** instead of keeping the original exactly as it was in every case.", "- Because the system works on the information it holds, what is remembered can differ from what was first experienced.", "- This is why memory is described as something that organizes and changes information, and not as a recording."]}, {"id": 4, "a": ["A **failure to use information** could reflect a problem at **more than one** of the three operations. Forgetting by itself therefore does not show which operation went wrong.", "- The information may never have been **encoded** well enough to be stored in a usable form.", "- It may not have been **retained in storage**, or it may be stored but not **retrievable** at that moment.", "- Telling these possibilities apart takes more than noticing that something was not remembered."]}, {"id": 5, "a": ["The **memory tree** distinguishes **sensory memory**, **short-term memory** and **long-term memory**.", "- Long-term memory then branches further, into declarative and procedural memory.", "- The classification distinguishes **functions**. It does not claim that all memories have the same duration and contents."]}, {"id": 6, "a": ["**Long-term memory** includes **declarative memory** and **procedural memory**. **Declarative memory** contains **episodic events** and **semantic knowledge**, so it is the part that holds events and facts.", "- **Procedural memory** concerns **skills**, which is the part of long-term memory about knowing how to do things.", "- Splitting long-term memory this way shows that it is not one uniform store but contains different kinds of memory."]}, {"id": 7, "a": ["**Episodic memory** and **semantic memory** are **branches of declarative memory**, which sits inside long-term memory. Episodic memory concerns events and semantic memory concerns knowledge.", "- They are **not additional short-term stores**. Both belong to long-term memory, because declarative memory is part of it.", "- The tree is therefore a map of kinds of memory, and not a list of separate temporary stores."]}, {"id": 8, "a": ["**Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968) modal model** distinguishes **three stores**: **sensory memory**, **short-term memory** and **long-term memory**.", "- Incoming information is described as passing through these stores, with sensory memory first, short-term memory next and long-term memory holding what lasts.", "- **Rehearsal** is the **control process** that can keep information available and support its transfer, so the model includes what the person does with information as well as where it is held."]}, {"id": 9, "a": ["The three stores differ in how long they hold information and how much. **Sensory memory briefly retains incoming stimulation for seconds or fractions of a second.**", "- **Short-term memory** is described as holding **five to seven items for about 15 to 20 seconds**. It is a small store, and what it holds is lost quickly unless it is kept available.", "- **Long-term memory** retains **large amounts for years or decades**, and it is the only store described as lasting years or decades."]}, {"id": 10, "a": ["**Rehearsal** is a **control process** in the modal model. It can **keep information available** and can **support its transfer** onward.", "- A control process is something the person does with information, which is different from the stores themselves.", "- For instance, when a phone number is repeated, the repetition keeps it available for immediate use and helps it become something that lasts."]}, {"id": 11, "a": ["Looking at a **directory** provides **many pieces of visual information at once**, and that input first enters **sensory memory** rather than being retained in full.", "- The store **briefly preserves** the information, and that brief preservation is what makes it possible for **attention to select relevant material** from it.", "- Not every detail is retained indefinitely, which fits the fact that a directory holds far more than anyone needs for one call."]}, {"id": 12, "a": ["**Sensory registration** and **durable learning** are **different outcomes**. Information can be registered in sensory memory for a moment and still never be learned.", "- Registration only means that the input entered the briefest store.", "- Durable learning means the information lasted beyond that moment, which requires more than being seen. This is why looking at a whole directory does not mean you remember it."]}, {"id": 13, "a": ["**Attention** is what moves information from the large sensory input into short-term memory. **Focusing on a particular phone number transfers the relevant information into short-term memory**, where it is available for immediate use.", "- The other directory contents do not have to remain accessible, because they were not selected.", "- **Selection limits which part of the large sensory input receives further processing.** What is attended is carried forward, and the rest does not have to remain accessible."]}, {"id": 14, "a": ["**Maintenance rehearsal** is **repeating the information**. Repeating the phone number keeps it **available in short-term memory long enough to make a call**.", "- It is a **control process** applied to the information that is already stored in short-term memory.", "- The repetition **prolongs immediate accessibility**, which would otherwise be vulnerable to **loss or interference**."]}, {"id": 15, "a": ["A **rehearsed number can become represented in long-term memory**. When that happens, later use **no longer depends on the directory still being visible**, because the number is now held independently of the source.", "- The modal model separates the **active short-term representation**, which is available for immediate use, from information **retained beyond the original encounter**.", "- Rehearsal is what supports this movement toward long-term storage."]}, {"id": 16, "a": ["The number can later be **retrieved from long-term memory into short-term memory for dialing**. **Remembering requires bringing stored information back into an active form.**", "- The interaction between the stores is therefore **not only one way**. New information moves toward long-term storage, and stored information moves back into short-term memory when it is needed.", "- Short-term memory is where the retrieved number becomes available for use."]}, {"id": 17, "a": ["**Sensory memory** briefly retains the effects of stimulation, and the traces differ by modality. An **icon** is a **fleeting visual representation**.", "- An **echo** is a **brief continuation of auditory information** after a sound has ended.", "- These sensory traces should **not be confused with the more durable representations** used to remember information much later, because they last only a very short time."]}, {"id": 18, "a": ["**Persistence of vision** is the **brief retention of the perception of light**. It **contributes to a sparkler’s trail**, where the moving light seems to leave a line, and to the **continuity experienced from film frames**.", "- Sensory memory holds **much information for a short interval**, which supports initial processing and **fills gaps between moments of input**.", "- Its large capacity does not imply long duration, because the information **decays quickly**."]}, {"id": 19, "a": ["No. **Sensory memory holds much information**, but a **large capacity does not imply long duration**. The information **decays quickly**.", "- That is why most of what registers is lost unless attention selects it for further processing.", "- Capacity and duration are separate properties, so a store can be very large and still last only a moment."]}, {"id": 20, "a": ["**Sperling (1960)** **flashed an array of letters** and asked participants to **report what they had seen**.", "- The question was whether the **limited report** reflected **limited initial registration** or **rapid loss before all the letters could be reported**.", "- **Changing the report requirement** allowed these explanations to be compared, because the two predict different results when the participants are asked for only part of the display."]}, {"id": 21, "a": ["Sperling (1960) compared three report conditions. In **whole report**, participants **report as many letters as possible**, which is the condition that produces the limited report.", "- In **partial report**, a **tone presented after the display** identifies **which row to recall**, so only one row is requested.", "- In **delayed partial report**, the **tone occurs after an additional delay**, which lets the experimenter see what happens when selection is postponed."]}, {"id": 22, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), the tone comes **after** the display, so the **row is not identified until after viewing**. Participants could not have decided in advance which row to memorize.", "- **Strong immediate partial report therefore implies that information about multiple rows was initially available.** Whichever row was cued could be reported.", "- The result separates what was available at first from what could be reported before it faded."]}, {"id": 23, "a": ["The array in Sperling’s (1960) task contained **X M L T, A F N B and C D Z P**. A **cue after the array selects a row for report** rather than telling the observer in advance which row to memorize.", "- **Increasing the cue delay allows the sensory trace to fade**, so the delay becomes a way to see how quickly the trace is lost.", "- The cue therefore asks about what was held in memory, and not about what the observer chose to focus on beforehand."]}, {"id": 24, "a": ["Sperling’s (1960) procedure **separates initial availability from the ability to report every item before it disappears**.", "- In whole report the two are mixed together, so a low score could reflect either too little registered or too little time to report it.", "- The cue lets availability be measured without requiring everything to be reported, which is what makes the comparison possible."]}, {"id": 25, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), **whole report averaged 4.5 of 12 letters, or 37.5 percent**.", "- **Immediate partial report averaged 3.3 of 4 letters, approximately 82 percent**, regardless of which row was selected.", "- **Delayed partial report averaged 1 of 4, or 25 percent**, in the comparison supplied.", "- The **immediate cue advantage** indicates that more information was initially available than whole report could recover."]}, {"id": 26, "a": ["No. The **whole-report limit of roughly four to five letters does not establish that only that many were perceived.**", "- In Sperling (1960), **immediate partial report permitted accurate report of whichever row was cued**, so more than that was available.", "- **Report limitations can therefore conceal greater initial availability.** The limit reflected what could be reported before the trace faded."]}, {"id": 27, "a": ["Sperling’s (1960) results show that **sensory memory has large capacity but rapid decay**. The large capacity is what lets any cued row be reported accurately.", "- **Most information is lost within about one second** unless it is **selected for further processing**.", "- The rapid decay is what makes whole report fall short, because the trace fades before all the letters can be named."]}, {"id": 28, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), **partial-report accuracy decreases as the cue is delayed** and **approaches whole-report performance**.", "- The **display is unchanged** and only the interval before selection varies, so the pattern links the loss to the delay.", "- This pattern links the immediate partial-report advantage to a **rapidly fading representation**. It **does not support indefinite retention** of the whole visual array."]}, {"id": 29, "a": ["**Short-term memory** retains **small amounts briefly** and is **sensitive to interruption and interference**. Anything that disturbs the person while information is held can disrupt it.", "- **Memory span** is the **amount that can be recalled accurately in a task**.", "- Span is a measurement of what a person can handle in a particular task, so it depends on the task and on the kind of material."]}, {"id": 30, "a": ["The **traditional estimate of memory span is seven plus or minus two units.**", "- The estimate **depends on the kind of units and the task**, so it is not a fixed property of the person.", "- It **should not be substituted for every visual-memory capacity result**, because other measurements, such as change detection, give different numbers."]}, {"id": 31, "a": ["**Recoding** **reorganizes or modifies information in short-term memory.**", "- It is a **control process** that acts on information while it is available, alongside rehearsal.", "- Because it changes the form of the information, it differs from simply repeating it."]}, {"id": 32, "a": ["**Maintenance rehearsal** **repeats information to prolong its availability.** It keeps something in mind for as long as the repetition continues.", "- **Elaborative rehearsal** **links new information with existing long-term knowledge**, which supports **longer-term learning**.", "- Repeating a label and explaining how it relates to what is already known are therefore **different control processes with different results**."]}, {"id": 33, "a": ["**Short-term memory** includes **selected sensory input** and **information retrieved from long-term memory.** It holds what is currently in use.", "- **Understanding sentences, doing arithmetic, dialing a number and navigating** all require keeping relevant information **currently accessible**.", "- It acts as a **window on the present**, and this active availability **differs from the large body of knowledge stored outside current awareness**."]}, {"id": 34, "a": ["**Clive Wearing** is a case of **memory impairment**. The case matters because it shows that **different memory functions need to be distinguished**.", "- Two of those functions are **maintaining immediate information** and **remembering across events**."]}, {"id": 35, "a": ["**Duration** concerns **how long information remains available**, while **capacity** concerns **how much can be held**.", "- They are separate properties, so a memory store can be described by both.", "- Sensory memory, for example, has a large capacity but a very short duration."]}, {"id": 36, "a": ["**Rehearsal can extend duration**, because repeating information keeps it available longer. **Chunking can extend effective capacity**, because grouping units lets more information be held.", "- Researchers therefore have to **account for these control processes when measuring the stores.**", "- Otherwise a result can reflect **the strategy used as well as the memory system being studied**."]}, {"id": 37, "a": ["**Brown (1958)** and **Peterson and Peterson (1959)** **presented three letters followed by a number.**", "- Participants **counted backward by threes during a delay of 3 to 18 seconds** and then **recalled the letters.**", "- Comparing the delays measures **retention when participants cannot simply keep repeating the trigram.**"]}, {"id": 38, "a": ["**Counting backward by threes prevents ordinary maintenance rehearsal.** In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), the counting fills the delay, so participants **cannot simply keep repeating the letters**.", "- That turns the delay into a test of how well the letters are retained when rehearsal is blocked.", "- Comparing different delays then shows how retention changes as time passes without rehearsal."]}, {"id": 39, "a": ["On the **first trial** in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), **recall is high at three seconds** and **remains relatively high at eighteen seconds.**", "- **No previous trigrams from the task compete with the current one**, so nothing earlier interferes.", "- The pattern **limits an interpretation that delay alone necessarily produces the full later decline**, since the delay was the same."]}, {"id": 40, "a": ["In **Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)**, By the **third trial**, **recall at eighteen seconds is lower than on the first trial.**", "- **Earlier trigrams now provide competing information.**", "- The same delay can therefore have **different effects depending on the preceding trials**, so **trial history is relevant** to interpreting short-term forgetting."]}, {"id": 41, "a": ["In **Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)**, after **many trials**, **recall at longer delays is substantially reduced.**", "- **Accumulated earlier material can interfere with the currently tested trigram.**", "- The **growing difference across trial positions** is evidence about **interference**, and not merely about the passage of eighteen seconds in isolation."]}, {"id": 42, "a": ["In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), the **average across trials produces a decline from shorter to longer delays.**", "- But that average **combines early trials with little interference and later trials with more interference**, so the overall curve can **hide important differences by trial position**.", "- An explanation has to account for **both the average and the changing pattern across trials**."]}, {"id": 43, "a": ["In **Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)**, the **percentage of letters recalled decreases with longer delays**, but the pattern **interacts with where a trial occurs in the sequence.** **Long-delay performance worsens as more trials are completed.**", "- **Delay and accumulated interference jointly affect retention.**", "- The finding **challenges interpreting every loss as pure time-based decay.**"]}, {"id": 44, "a": ["**Decay** refers to **loss associated with the passage of time.** **Proactive interference** occurs when **earlier information disrupts remembering newer information.**", "- The Brown–Peterson pattern includes evidence for interference because **earlier trials affect later recall.**", "- So the loss cannot be explained by elapsed time alone."]}, {"id": 45, "a": ["The effective duration of short-term memory is given as about **15 to 20 seconds.**", "- The observed outcome depends on **rehearsal and interference conditions**, so the number is not fixed regardless of what the person is doing."]}, {"id": 46, "a": ["**Interference** occurs when **memories compete with or replace one another.** **Forgetting is therefore not explained solely by elapsed time.**", "- **Retroactive interference** and **proactive interference** distinguish the **direction of the competition.**", "- To label the effect correctly, **identify which information is being recalled** and whether the competing information came earlier or later."]}, {"id": 47, "a": ["**Retroactive interference** occurs when **new information disrupts memory for older information.**", "- **Learning a new phone number can make the old number harder to retrieve.** The **affected memory is the old one**, and the **interfering information arrived later.**"]}, {"id": 48, "a": ["**Proactive interference** occurs when **old information disrupts memory for newer information.**", "- **Previous parking locations can interfere with remembering where the car was parked today.** The older memories compete with the newer one.", "- **Routine events are especially vulnerable** because similar memories compete."]}, {"id": 49, "a": ["**Both interference types can be reduced when events are distinctive.**", "- Distinctive events compete less with one another, because they are easier to tell apart.", "- Routine events with similar memories are the most vulnerable, since many alike memories compete at once."]}, {"id": 50, "a": ["**Digit span** measures the **longest sequence of digits recalled correctly in order.** **Increasing sequence length makes the task progressively more demanding.**", "- **Capacity differs between individuals and can be affected by strategies.**", "- **Accurate memory for the digits without their order does not satisfy an ordered-span task**, because the order is part of what has to be right."]}, {"id": 51, "a": ["The digit lists **increase in length from four items to ten.** The **longest row reproduced completely and in order defines the measured span.**", "- **One successful short list does not establish the upper limit**, because longer lists may still be recalled.", "- **One missed digit makes a longer list incorrect** under this scoring rule."]}, {"id": 52, "a": ["**Miller’s 1956 paper, The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two**, proposed an **influential capacity description.**", "- The **units being remembered can differ across tasks.**", "- The title should **not be treated as proof that every short-term system stores exactly seven raw sensory elements.**"]}, {"id": 53, "a": ["No. **Miller’s (1956) estimate applies to units such as digits, words, numbers or phrases**, and **a unit can contain different amounts of information.**", "- The number is therefore a count of units, and not of equal pieces of raw sensory information.", "- This is the point that **Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)** build on when they ask about the amount of information in each item."]}, {"id": 54, "a": ["**Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)** **compared colored squares with more complex objects using change detection.**", "- Their **alternative emphasis is on the amount or complexity of information** rather than **a fixed number of equally sized items.**", "- Capacity therefore depends on what the items are, and not only on how many there are."]}, {"id": 55, "a": ["**Visual short-term memory, VSTM,** is **measured by comparing displays after a delay.**", "- Its **capacity can be smaller than span for digit lists or unrelated words.**", "- **Researchers debate how many modality- or domain-specific stores are needed**, and different tasks do not have to measure one identical capacity."]}, {"id": 56, "a": ["In a change-detection task, participants **view an initial array, marked S1, briefly.** In the sequence described its duration is **100 milliseconds.**", "- They must **retain relevant visual properties after it disappears.**", "- The later response requires **a comparison with memory, not simultaneous inspection of two visible arrays.**"]}, {"id": 57, "a": ["**Once a display disappears, its colors and shapes are no longer available directly.**", "- A **short-term representation must preserve the features needed for comparison.**", "- **Color, shape, orientation and complexity** can affect **how demanding that retention is.**"]}, {"id": 58, "a": ["The **second display, S2,** appears for **2,000 milliseconds.** Participants **judge whether the display is the same or different.**", "- **Correct performance requires comparing S2 with the retained S1 representation.**", "- **Response accuracy therefore depends on memory for the first display.**"]}, {"id": 59, "a": ["**Change-detection accuracy falls sharply when set size exceeds roughly four items.** The conclusion is a **capacity of about three to four items in visual working memory.**", "- This **differs from the traditional seven-plus-or-minus-two estimate** for other tasks.", "- The comparison **depends on stimulus type, delay and the kind of response required.**"]}, {"id": 60, "a": ["**Same–different performance depends on stimulus complexity.** **Colored squares support higher estimated capacity than more complex polygons or shaded cubes.**", "- **Treating all stimuli as equally sized items can hide these differences.**", "- **Visual-memory capacity is therefore constrained by the information required to represent the objects.**"]}, {"id": 61, "a": ["The **complexity comparison also includes Chinese-character-like forms.** **Familiarity and the structure attributed to a stimulus can affect what counts as a unit of information.**", "- Capacity differences must be **interpreted relative to the particular stimulus classes used.**", "- **A bar for one category is not a universal capacity estimate for all visual objects.**"]}, {"id": 62, "a": ["**Chunking** **combines smaller units into larger meaningful units.**", "- **Ericsson and colleagues (1980)** **trained a student to reach a digit span of 79**, using **familiar running times** to organize the sequences.", "- The gain **depends on knowledge-based organization** rather than **expanding every raw memory limit equally.**"]}, {"id": 63, "a": ["**Chase and Simon (1973)** found that **chess expertise supports grouping meaningful positions.**", "- The gain depends on **knowledge-based organization** and not on a larger raw memory limit.", "- Chunking in chess works because the pieces form familiar relationships that an expert recognizes as a unit."]}, {"id": 64, "a": ["In Chase and Simon (1973), **chess masters recalled more correct piece placements than beginners for meaningful game arrangements.**", "- **The advantage is greatly reduced for random arrangements.**", "- **Expertise supports chunks representing familiar relationships among pieces.** **Randomizing those relationships removes much of the benefit while retaining the same general kinds of pieces.**"]}, {"id": 65, "a": ["**Control processes** include **rehearsal, strategies for making material memorable, and directing attention.**", "- The **memory palace** is an example of **organizing information through a remembered spatial structure.**", "- Considering these **active operations** leads toward **working memory**, which concerns **using and managing currently available information.**"]}, {"id": 66, "a": ["**Attention** selects part of the large **sensory input** for **short-term memory**, as when focusing on a phone number moves it forward.", "- **Rehearsal** then **keeps the information available** and can support its transfer onward to **long-term memory.**", "- **Retrieval** brings information from **long-term memory back into short-term memory**, as when a stored number is retrieved for dialing.", "- The stores are therefore connected by control processes in both directions, and not by a one-way flow."]}, {"id": 67, "a": ["**Sensory memory** has a **large capacity but decays quickly.** **Most information is lost within about one second** unless it is selected for further processing.", "- **Short-term memory** holds **only a small amount**, **five to seven items**, for about **15 to 20 seconds.**", "- The contrast is large capacity with a very short duration for sensory memory, against small capacity with a somewhat longer duration for short-term memory."]}, {"id": 68, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), **whole report averaged only 4.5 of 12 letters**, but **immediate partial report averaged about 82 percent for whichever row was cued.**", "- Because the **row was cued after the display**, far more than four or five letters must have been available at first.", "- The **limit therefore came from rapid decay before everything could be reported**, and not from what was perceived."]}, {"id": 69, "a": ["Both Sperling (1960) and the Brown–Peterson task **vary the time before report** and test what happens as time passes. In Sperling’s task, **delaying the cue** lets the **sensory trace fade**, and partial report falls toward whole report.", "- In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), the **delay of 3 to 18 seconds is filled with counting backward** so rehearsal is blocked, and recall of the letters is tested.", "- One delay tests the fading of a sensory trace, and the other tests retention in short-term memory without rehearsal."]}, {"id": 70, "a": ["**Decay** is **loss associated with the passage of time.** **Interference** is **memories competing with or replacing one another.**", "- The Brown–Peterson pattern shows that **forgetting is not explained solely by elapsed time**, because **earlier trials affect later recall.**", "- **Delay and accumulated interference jointly affect retention.**"]}, {"id": 71, "a": ["The Brown–Peterson pattern shows **proactive interference**, because **earlier trigrams disrupt recall of the newer one.**", "- **Retroactive interference** runs the other way: **new information disrupts memory for older information**, as when a new phone number makes the old one harder to retrieve.", "- To label it correctly, identify **which information is being recalled** and whether the competing information came earlier or later."]}, {"id": 72, "a": ["**Maintenance rehearsal** **prolongs availability** of information in short-term memory by repeating it.", "- **Elaborative rehearsal** **links new information with existing long-term knowledge**, which **supports longer-term learning.**", "- One keeps information available for now, and the other helps it last."]}, {"id": 73, "a": ["**Duration** is how long information stays available and **capacity** is how much can be held. **Rehearsal can extend duration** and **chunking can extend effective capacity.**", "- A measurement can therefore reflect **the strategy used as well as the memory system being studied.**", "- Researchers must account for these **control processes** when they measure the stores."]}, {"id": 74, "a": ["There are several estimates. The modal model describes **five to seven items**, the traditional estimate is **seven plus or minus two units**, and visual short-term memory is about **three to four items.**", "- Each depends on **the kind of units and the task**, and **the traditional estimate should not be substituted for every visual-memory result.**", "- Stimulus complexity also changes the estimate, so there is **no single capacity number.**"]}, {"id": 75, "a": ["**Digit span** measures **the longest sequence of digits recalled correctly in order**, so it depends on **ordered recall** of a list that grows longer.", "- **Change detection** compares a **second display with a retained first display after a delay**, and it asks only whether they are the **same or different.**", "- The tasks use different stimuli and responses, which is why their capacity estimates differ."]}, {"id": 76, "a": ["**Miller (1956)** describes capacity in **units**, such as **digits, words, numbers or phrases**, and the units can differ across tasks.", "- **Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)** emphasize **the amount or complexity of information** in each item, comparing **colored squares with more complex objects.**", "- So the first counts units, and the second says that a unit can carry more or less information."]}, {"id": 77, "a": ["In Chase and Simon (1973), **chess masters recalled more correct placements for meaningful game arrangements** because they could group pieces into **chunks representing familiar relationships.**", "- **Random arrangements remove those relationships**, so the **advantage is greatly reduced.**", "- The pieces are the same, so the difference comes from **knowledge-based organization**, and not from a larger memory."]}, {"id": 78, "a": ["**Ericsson and colleagues (1980)** trained a student to reach a **digit span of 79** by using **familiar running times** to organize sequences. **Chase and Simon (1973)** found that **chess expertise supports grouping meaningful positions.**", "- In both, **knowledge-based organization** raises what can be remembered, and not an expansion of every raw memory limit equally.", "- In one the knowledge is about running times, and in the other it is about chess relationships."]}, {"id": 79, "a": ["**Encoding, storage and retrieval** are **operations on information**, while **sensory, short-term and long-term memory** are the **stores.**", "- Information is **encoded** into a usable form, **retained** in a store, and **retrieved** back into an active form.", "- The phone number shows this: it moves into short-term memory, is stored in long-term memory, and is **retrieved from long-term memory into short-term memory for dialing.**"]}, {"id": 80, "a": ["The **memory tree** distinguishes **sensory, short-term and long-term memory**, with **long-term memory** including **declarative** and **procedural** memory. The **modal model** distinguishes the **same three stores** and adds **rehearsal** as a control process.", "- They overlap in treating **sensory, short-term and long-term memory** as distinct.", "- The tree emphasizes the **kinds of long-term memory**, and the modal model emphasizes **how information moves between stores.**"]}, {"id": 81, "a": ["An **icon** is a **fleeting visual representation** held in sensory memory. **Persistence of vision** is the **brief retention of the perception of light** that **contributes to a sparkler’s trail** and to the **continuity experienced from film frames.**", "- Both involve **very brief retention**, and **neither is a durable representation** used to remember information much later."]}, {"id": 82, "a": ["Different memory functions need to be distinguished, such as **maintaining immediate information** and **remembering across events.**", "- In a case like **Clive Wearing**, **a case name by itself does not specify every affected memory system or every preserved ability.**", "- Analyzing the case **function by function** avoids assuming that one memory system explains everything."]}, {"id": 83, "a": ["**Short-term memory** includes **selected sensory input** and **information retrieved from long-term memory.** Current thought therefore draws on **both.**", "- Dialing a number uses **retrieval from long-term memory into short-term memory**, so the **window on the present** depends on stored knowledge as well as new input."]}, {"id": 84, "a": ["**Control processes** include **rehearsal, strategies for making material memorable and directing attention.** These are **active operations on available information.**", "- **Working memory** concerns **using and managing currently available information**, so thinking about these operations leads directly to it."]}, {"id": 85, "a": ["In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), **counting backward by threes prevents ordinary maintenance rehearsal**, so retention is measured **without simple repetition.**", "- **Chunking extends effective capacity**, so a result can reflect the strategy as well as the store.", "- Researchers must **account for control processes such as rehearsal and chunking** when they measure the stores."]}, {"id": 86, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), the **whole-report limit hid the larger initial availability**, which only the **cued partial report** revealed.", "- In change detection (Alvarez and Cavanagh, 2004), the **capacity estimate depends on stimulus type, delay and the response required.**", "- In both, **the way capacity is measured shapes what appears to be stored.**"]}, {"id": 87, "a": ["The modal model’s view of **short-term memory** is **too simple if temporary memory is treated as passive storage.** Keeping information available also involves **control processes**, which are **operations that act on that information.**", "- A memory model has to account for **both holding something in mind and doing something with it.**", "- Passive storage only describes the holding, and it leaves out the operations that act on what is held."]}, {"id": 88, "a": ["**Baddeley and Hitch (1974)** proposed **working memory** as a **limited-capacity system for the temporary storage and manipulation of information.**", "- **Temporary storage** keeps information available for immediate use.", "- **Manipulation** changes or works with that information while it remains available. Working memory therefore includes active processing and does not treat it as separate from temporary memory."]}, {"id": 89, "a": ["**Comprehension, learning and reasoning** can require **storage and manipulation together.** **Comprehending material** involves keeping information available while relating its parts.", "- **Reasoning** involves **maintaining the information used in a decision** while performing the operations that lead to an answer.", "- In both, something has to be held in mind and worked on at the same time, which is what working memory provides."]}, {"id": 90, "a": ["In Baddeley and Hitch’s (1974) definition, **manipulation** means **changing or working with information while it remains available.**", "- It is paired with **temporary storage**, which **keeps information available for immediate use.**", "- Storage holds the information, and manipulation operates on it, so the definition covers both holding and doing."]}, {"id": 91, "a": ["Multiplying **43 times 6 in your head** requires **both storage and active processing.** First, **3 × 6 = 18.** The **8 is held in memory as the ones digit**, and the **1 is carried over the 4.**", "- **Holding the 8 is storage.** **Carrying the 1 is an operation** performed on the information being used.", "- Next, **6 × 4 = 24**, and **adding the carried 1 gives 25.** Combining that **25 with the 8 held in memory produces 258.**", "- The 8 has to survive while another multiplication and an addition are carried out."]}, {"id": 92, "a": ["Working memory accounts for **keeping an intermediate result available while changing other parts of the problem.**", "- A system that only holds information would describe the retained 8, but the calculation also requires **coordinating which number is stored, which number is carried, and which operation is performed.**", "- The digits have to remain accessible even when attention is directed toward one part of the multiplication."]}, {"id": 93, "a": ["In **Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968)** short-term memory model, **a single component holds different types of information for a short time.** The emphasis is on **temporary availability** and not on separate systems for different forms of processing.", "- **Baddeley and Hitch’s (1974)** working-memory model **separates the system into the central executive, the visuospatial sketchpad and the phonological loop.**", "- **Visual and spatial information** can be maintained in a different component from **verbal and auditory information**, and the **central executive coordinates their use.**"]}, {"id": 94, "a": ["The **episodic buffer** is a **fourth component added by Baddeley in 2000.** It **belongs to the expanded model** and not to the original three-component proposal.", "- The model therefore distinguishes **specialized temporary stores, executive control, and a component that integrates information.**"]}, {"id": 95, "a": ["The **phonological loop** **maintains verbal and auditory information.** It has two parts.", "- The **phonological store** holds the information.", "- The **articulatory rehearsal process** keeps it active through rehearsal. **Repeating verbal material refreshes information that would otherwise decay in the store.**"]}, {"id": 96, "a": ["**Articulatory** refers to **producing speech.** In the loop, rehearsal can involve **repeating the sounds of the material internally** rather than speaking them aloud.", "- The **store holds the verbal information**, and the **rehearsal process acts on it** to keep it available.", "- Without that repetition, the information would fade from the store."]}, {"id": 97, "a": ["The **visuospatial sketchpad** holds **visual and spatial information.** **Visual information** concerns **the appearance of something.**", "- **Spatial information** concerns **where its parts are located and how they are arranged.**", "- These are **different demands from maintaining the sounds of words**."]}, {"id": 98, "a": ["The **central executive** **pulls information from long-term memory, coordinates the other components, and directs and maintains attention.**", "- It **controls how the temporary stores contribute to the current task.**", "- It is **not another store for a particular kind of information**, so it differs in kind from the loop and the sketchpad."]}, {"id": 99, "a": ["The **central executive, phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad** are **interacting parts** and not a single undifferentiated short-term store. **Sensory memory** receives incoming information, and working memory operates on information available for the task.", "- The **connections between the components allow a task to use more than one kind of representation.**", "- **Long-term knowledge** is connected to the components, so **information already known can be made available during a current task**, with the central executive coordinating its use."]}, {"id": 100, "a": ["A calculation can involve **verbal rehearsal of a result together with a visual representation of the digits.**", "- The **phonological loop supports verbal and auditory information**, while the **visuospatial sketchpad supports visual and spatial information.**", "- The **connections between the components** are what let one task use both kinds of representation."]}, {"id": 101, "a": ["The **phonological similarity effect** occurs when **letters or words that sound similar are confused.** **Phonological** refers to **the sounds of language.**", "- Items can be **difficult to keep distinct when their sound patterns overlap**, **even when their written forms differ.**", "- The effect concerns **confusion between sound patterns.**"]}, {"id": 102, "a": ["The **word-length effect** means that **memory for lists is better for short words than for long words.**", "- **Longer words take more time to rehearse and more time to produce during recall.**", "- **Maintaining a list therefore depends partly on how quickly its verbal contents can be repeated.**"]}, {"id": 103, "a": ["**Articulatory suppression** involves **speaking while trying to remember other material.**", "- **Speaking occupies the rehearsal process**, so it **interferes with the repetition that normally keeps verbal items available.**", "- **Memory is reduced because the competing speech uses the process needed for rehearsal.**"]}, {"id": 104, "a": ["The three effects **concern different aspects of verbal maintenance.** **Similarity concerns confusion between sound patterns**, **word length concerns the time required to repeat items**, and **suppression concerns interference with the rehearsal process itself.**", "- Each depends on how the phonological loop keeps verbal information available."]}, {"id": 105, "a": ["**Conrad (1964)** **briefly flashed target letters** for participants to see, and then **asked them to write the letters down.**", "- **In half of the presentations the letters were similar, and in half they were not.**", "- The task required **retaining letter identities after a brief visual presentation.**"]}, {"id": 106, "a": ["In **Conrad (1964)**, participants’ errors were **more likely to substitute a letter that sounded like the target than one that merely looked like it.**", "- **An E was likely to be replaced with a B**, whose spoken name sounds similar.", "- **A visually similar substitution such as F for E was less likely**, even though the letters had been seen and not heard, which is what points to sound-based maintenance."]}, {"id": 107, "a": ["In **Conrad (1964)**, **Visually presented letters can be recoded into a sound-based representation.** The letters entered through vision, but the **confusions followed their spoken names.**", "- **Remembering a seen letter can involve converting it into a sound-based representation**, and the **phonological loop contributes to that conversion** as well as to holding the result.", "- The **E–B confusion** is evidence of sound-based maintenance because the overlap is in **pronunciation** and not in the visible shapes."]}, {"id": 108, "a": ["**Presentation format** is **what participants see.** The **memory code** is **the form in which the information is being maintained.**", "- In Conrad (1964), the letters were presented visually, but the **sound-based substitutions** show that they were maintained as sounds.", "- **Visually presented letters can consequently be confused according to pronunciation rather than appearance.**"]}, {"id": 109, "a": ["**Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984)** found that **people remembered more short words than long words.**", "- The **word-length effect concerns how long the items take to pronounce and rehearse.**", "- **A list of longer words takes more time to repeat**, leaving **fewer words available within the same brief rehearsal period.**"]}, {"id": 110, "a": ["**Ellis and Hennelly (1980)** found that **Welsh–English bilingual children had a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English.**", "- **Digit span** is the **number of digits that can be retained and reported in order.**", "- The children knew both languages, but **Welsh number names took longer to pronounce.** The difference follows the **time needed to say the number names** and not the numerical values."]}, {"id": 111, "a": ["**Span is related to the amount of verbal material that can be said in about 1.5 to 2 seconds.**", "- **Faster rehearsal allows more items to be refreshed within that interval.**", "- **Longer pronunciation time reduces the number of separate items that can be kept active together.**"]}, {"id": 112, "a": ["When a person speaks while memorizing, the **competing speech keeps the phonological loop busy.**", "- The person has **less opportunity to rehearse the items that must be remembered**, so **recall becomes worse.**", "- The interference is consistent with a system that uses **speech-related rehearsal** to maintain verbal information."]}, {"id": 113, "a": ["The **word-length effect is reduced during suppression.** **Short words normally have an advantage because they can be rehearsed more quickly than long words.**", "- When the rehearsal process is occupied by another speaking task, **that advantage is reduced** because neither list can receive ordinary rehearsal."]}, {"id": 114, "a": ["The **phonological similarity effect is also reduced** under articulatory suppression.", "- **Verbal rehearsal and the conversion of seen items into their sounds are disrupted**, so the **sound-based overlap between the remembered items has less influence** on performance."]}, {"id": 115, "a": ["**Visual information** includes **the appearance and shape of something**, what it looks like.", "- **Spatial information** includes **the arrangement and relative locations of its parts**, where things are in relation to one another.", "- Spatial information **allows that arrangement to remain available without continuous visual input**, so the arrangement can be held in mind after the object is gone. Both kinds are held by the **visuospatial sketchpad.**"]}, {"id": 116, "a": ["The **visuospatial sketchpad supports mental imagery.** **Visual imagery involves keeping a visual representation in mind**, so the **represented object can be used during a task even when it is no longer being viewed.**", "- **Maintaining its shape and the positions of its features places demands on the sketchpad.**", "- The sketchpad is **distinct from the phonological loop**, so holding a shape in mind is a different demand from holding the sounds of a sentence."]}, {"id": 117, "a": ["In **Brooks (1968)**, participants **memorized a sentence and decided, for each word, whether it was or was not a noun.**", "- The words of the sentence had to **remain available while participants classified them one by one.**", "- **In one condition participants indicated decisions by speaking, and in another by pointing.**"]}, {"id": 118, "a": ["In Brooks (1968), **pointing was easier than speaking.** **Maintaining and processing the sentence occupied the phonological loop**, and **speaking the classifications also required verbal processing**, creating competition.", "- **Pointing used a different response format while the visuospatial sketchpad was available.**", "- **The difficulty depended on the combination of what was remembered and how the answer was produced.**"]}, {"id": 119, "a": ["In Brooks (1968), participants **memorized the shape of an F**, **mentally followed its outline and classified each corner as an inside corner or an outside corner.**", "- **An inside corner turns into an indentation of the shape, while an outside corner projects around its outer boundary.**", "- Participants either **spoke their classifications or pointed to the OUT and IN response alternatives.**"]}, {"id": 120, "a": ["In Brooks (1968), **speaking was easier than pointing** in the F task.", "- **Holding and following the F used the visuospatial sketchpad**, and **pointing to spatially arranged responses added a competing spatial demand.**", "- **The phonological loop was available to support spoken responses**, so speaking competed less. The advantage is the opposite of the sentence-task result because the remembered information is **visual and spatial** and not verbal."]}, {"id": 121, "a": ["In **Brooks (1968)**, Tasks are easier when **the information held in mind and the operation performed on it involve different types of short-term memory.**", "- For a sentence, **speaking competes** and pointing is easier. For a shape, **pointing competes** and speaking is easier.", "- The **opposite response advantages support some independence between verbal or phonological memory and visual or spatial memory.**"]}, {"id": 122, "a": ["**Shepard and Metzler (1971)** **presented pairs of block-built objects at different orientations.**", "- Participants **judged whether the objects matched despite the difference in orientation.**", "- The task requires **comparing the arrangement of parts** and not judging that two pictures differ merely because one is tilted."]}, {"id": 123, "a": ["**Mental rotation** is **turning a representation in mind to compare how its parts line up.**", "- **Picture-plane rotation changes an object’s orientation within the flat viewing plane**, and **depth rotation changes it in three-dimensional space.**", "- In either case the task requires **comparing the arrangement of parts.**"]}, {"id": 124, "a": ["In Shepard and Metzler (1971), for the picture-plane pairs, **reaction time increased as the angle of rotation increased**, and the **increase was approximately linear.**", "- **A larger angular separation requires a larger mental transformation before the objects can be compared in alignment.**", "- The pattern is consistent with **an active operation on a spatial representation**, and not just storage of an unchanged picture."]}, {"id": 125, "a": ["The **central executive** does the **work of working memory by directing and maintaining attention.** It supports **focusing on relevant information, dividing attention across demands, and switching attention when the task changes.**", "- It also includes **suppressing irrelevant information, coordinating the sketchpad and the phonological loop, and performing calculations.**", "- These are **operations that control processing** and not a separate type of stored content."]}, {"id": 126, "a": ["**Miyake and colleagues (2000)** distinguish **updating, shifting and inhibition.**", "- **Updating changes the information currently maintained.**", "- **Shifting changes the task or operation being performed.**", "- **Inhibition suppresses information or responses that interfere with the current goal.**"]}, {"id": 127, "a": ["The central-executive idea is sometimes criticized as a **homunculus**, meaning **a little internal agent assigned the job of controlling everything else.**", "- **Naming a controller does not itself explain how control is performed.**", "- **Separating updating, shifting and inhibition specifies different operations** within what would otherwise be one broad executive label."]}, {"id": 128, "a": ["**Focusing** is concentrating on **relevant information**, **dividing attention** is spreading it **across demands**, and **switching attention** is changing it **when the task changes.**", "- All three are **operations that control processing** and are part of how the central executive directs and maintains attention."]}, {"id": 129, "a": ["**Baddeley (2000)** added the **episodic buffer** as a **fourth working-memory component.** It has a **limited capacity of about 4 chunks.**", "- A **chunk is a unit that combines related information**, so the capacity concerns **organized units** and not necessarily four individual words."]}, {"id": 130, "a": ["The **episodic buffer binds information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes.**", "- **Binding means keeping different kinds of information together as a connected representation.**", "- **Verbal information, visual or spatial information, and existing knowledge** can consequently contribute to one remembered episode."]}, {"id": 131, "a": ["**People can immediately recall about 16 words from a meaningful sentence**, which exceeds their **span for unrelated words.** **Meaning allows the words to be retained as connected material**, and long-term language knowledge contributes to that organization.", "- **Some amnesic patients can immediately recall a just-heard story**, so **immediate retention of an organized episode can be present even when longer-term memory is impaired.**", "- The buffer accounts for **temporary integrated representations that extend beyond the contents of the separate verbal and spatial stores.**"]}, {"id": 132, "a": ["**Vogel and colleagues (2005)** first **measured participants’ working-memory capacity** and distinguished **higher-capacity and lower-capacity groups.**", "- Participants then did a **visual memory task while the researchers measured event-related potentials, or ERPs.**", "- An **ERP is an electrical brain response measured in relation to a task event.**"]}, {"id": 133, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), **a cue directed attention to one side of a display.** Participants **briefly saw red bars whose orientations had to be remembered**, followed by a **retention interval with the bars absent**, and then a **test display.**", "- Their task was to **judge whether a relevant orientation had changed.**", "- The **memory display lasted 100 milliseconds**, and the **retention interval lasted 900 milliseconds.**"]}, {"id": 134, "a": ["In **Vogel and colleagues (2005)**, **the more complex displays included irrelevant blue bars alongside the relevant red bars.** The **red-bar information remained the memory target.**", "- **Ignoring the blue bars required selection within the display** and not simply remembering everything visible.", "- **Comparing target-only and distractor conditions tested how irrelevant items entered working-memory processing.**"]}, {"id": 135, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), **the electrical memory-related response continues into the retention interval**, **after the memory display has disappeared.**", "- The response has a **negative electrical polarity**, so a **larger memory-related response means a stronger negative deflection** and not simply a more positive voltage."]}, {"id": 136, "a": ["For **high-capacity participants** in Vogel and colleagues (2005), **the response is greater with four relevant items than with two.**", "- **Adding irrelevant blue items to two relevant items produces little change.** The distractor condition remains **close to the two-target condition.**", "- The additional visible objects **are not accompanied by the increase associated with maintaining four relevant objects.**"]}, {"id": 137, "a": ["For **low-capacity participants** in Vogel and colleagues (2005), **adding the irrelevant items increases the response toward the four-item condition.**", "- Their **working-memory processing includes more of the irrelevant material.**", "- **Effective capacity is therefore related to which items enter memory as well as to the number of targets presented.**"]}, {"id": 138, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), **working-memory capacity is positively correlated with filtering efficiency.**", "- **Participants with higher capacity tend to have higher filtering-efficiency values.**", "- **Filtering efficiency concerns how effectively irrelevant items are excluded from working-memory processing.**"]}, {"id": 139, "a": ["In **Vogel and colleagues (2005)**, **people with higher measured capacity are also less distracted by irrelevant items.**", "- **A lower memory score can reflect the entry of competing material**, which occupies processing that would otherwise be available for targets.", "- **Individual differences in working memory include differences in selection and control** as well as in how much relevant information is retained."]}, {"id": 140, "a": ["The **modal model’s short-term memory** is **a single component that holds different types of information for a short time**, emphasizing **temporary availability.**", "- **Working memory** has **separate components for verbal, visual–spatial and integrated information**, plus **executive control**, and it includes **manipulation** of information.", "- It is therefore both a store and a system for operating on what is held."]}, {"id": 141, "a": ["In **43 × 6**, **the retained 8 is storage**, but **carrying the 1 and performing the next multiplication and addition are operations.**", "- The system must **both hold and manipulate information**, which is why a single passive store does not describe the task."]}, {"id": 142, "a": ["The **phonological loop maintains verbal and auditory information**, and the **visuospatial sketchpad maintains visual and spatial information.**", "- The **central executive is not a store.** It **coordinates both, directs attention and draws on long-term memory.**", "- The loop and the sketchpad differ by what they hold, and the executive differs in what it does."]}, {"id": 143, "a": ["**Similarity concerns confusion between sound patterns, word length concerns the time required to repeat items, and suppression concerns interference with the rehearsal process itself.**", "- They are **different aspects of verbal maintenance.**", "- **Suppression**, which occupies rehearsal, also **reduces the word-length and similarity effects**, which ties the three together."]}, {"id": 144, "a": ["In **Conrad (1964)**, the letters were **seen**, but **confusions followed sound, such as E replaced by B.**", "- The **maintained code was therefore phonological and not visual.**", "- **Presentation format** (what was seen) differs from **memory code** (how it was kept)."]}, {"id": 145, "a": ["**Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984)** found **more short words than long words** were remembered, and **Ellis and Hennelly (1980)** found a **shorter digit span in Welsh than in English.**", "- Both show that **span depends on how long items take to say.**", "- Long words and Welsh number names take longer to rehearse, so **fewer items fit in about 1.5 to 2 seconds.**"]}, {"id": 146, "a": ["The **phonological store holds verbal and auditory information**, the **visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information**, and the **episodic buffer binds information from the loop, the sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes.**", "- Each component holds a **different kind of information**, and the buffer is the one that **combines** them."]}, {"id": 147, "a": ["In **Brooks (1968)**, the **sentence task was easier with pointing** and the **F task was easier with speaking.**", "- In each case, the **response was easier when it used a different type of memory from the one holding the information.**", "- That shows that **verbal and visual–spatial memory are partly independent.**"]}, {"id": 148, "a": ["**Brooks (1968)** varied whether the **response used speaking or pointing**, and found it was **easier when it used a different type of memory from the one holding the information.**", "- **Vogel and colleagues (2005)** added **irrelevant blue bars**, and found that **higher-capacity participants kept them out.**", "- In both, a **competing or irrelevant demand** reveals how working memory handles interference."]}, {"id": 149, "a": ["In **Shepard and Metzler (1971)**, **response time rose at an approximately linear rate with the angle of rotation.** A larger angular separation required a larger mental transformation.", "- That pattern is **consistent with an active operation on a spatial representation**, and not just storage of an unchanged picture.", "- **Working memory is described as both storage and manipulation**, so mental rotation is an example of manipulation, turning a held representation to compare its parts."]}, {"id": 150, "a": ["The **stores** hold contents: the **phonological loop** holds **verbal or auditory information**, and the **visuospatial sketchpad** holds **visual and spatial information.**", "- The **central executive** does the **work**: it **directs and maintains attention**, **suppresses irrelevant information**, **coordinates the sketchpad and the phonological loop**, and **pulls information from long-term memory.** It is **not another store for a particular kind of information.**", "- **Updating, shifting and inhibition** from **Miyake and colleagues (2000)** describe operations of control: **changing what is maintained**, **changing the task**, and **suppressing interference.**"]}, {"id": 151, "a": ["**Naming a controller does not itself explain how control is performed**, which is the **homunculus** objection.", "- **Separating updating, shifting and inhibition in Miyake and colleagues (2000) specifies different operations** within what would otherwise be one broad executive label."]}, {"id": 152, "a": ["**People recall about 16 words of a meaningful sentence**, beyond their **span for unrelated words**, and **amnesic patients can immediately recall a story.**", "- These findings suggest **an integrated temporary store that uses long-term knowledge**, which the **loop and the sketchpad alone do not provide.**", "- That is the role of the **episodic buffer.**"]}, {"id": 153, "a": ["The buffer’s capacity is **about four chunks**, which are **organized units and not necessarily four individual words.**", "- **Meaningful material can exceed the span for unrelated words**, which fits the finding that about 16 words of a sentence can be recalled."]}, {"id": 154, "a": ["**Differences in capacity include selection and control.**", "- The **executive’s functions include suppressing irrelevant information and directing attention.**", "- In Vogel and colleagues (2005), **higher-capacity participants kept distractors out**, which is a matter of selection."]}, {"id": 155, "a": ["In **Vogel and colleagues (2005)**, **high-capacity participants kept blue distractors out**, while **low-capacity participants let them in.**", "- **Effective capacity therefore depends on selection**, meaning which items get in, and not just how many items are shown."]}, {"id": 156, "a": ["**Conrad’s (1964)** letter confusions show **phonological loop coding**: letters that were seen were maintained as sounds and confused by their spoken names.", "- **Brooks’s (1968)** tasks show **competition within a component.** In the sentence task, **speaking competes with the held sentence in the phonological loop**, and in the F task, **pointing competes with the held shape in the visuospatial sketchpad.**", "- Conrad’s result is about the code used for letters, and Brooks’s result is about which responses interfere with which held information."]}, {"id": 157, "a": ["In **Shepard and Metzler (1971)**, **reaction time rose with the angle of rotation**, which fits **an operation on a spatial representation**: the sketchpad holds a shape and a person turns it in mind.", "- In **Brooks (1968)**, **holding the shape of an F used the visuospatial sketchpad**, so **pointing to spatially arranged responses added a competing spatial demand.**", "- Both show the sketchpad holding visual and spatial information, one by what can be done with it and the other by what interferes with it."]}, {"id": 158, "a": ["**A single short-term store keeps information temporarily available**, with the emphasis on **temporary availability.** It describes holding and not working on information.", "- **Working memory adds operations performed on held information**, as in **calculation, reasoning and comprehension**, with **separate components, the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad and the central executive**, and later an episodic buffer.", "- It is therefore both a place to hold information and a system for **manipulation**, which **43 × 6** shows: the 8 is held while the carrying and the next multiplication are performed."]}, {"id": 159, "a": ["**Conrad (1964)** found that **seen letters were confused by their spoken names.**", "- **Ellis and Hennelly (1980)** found that **digit span was shorter in Welsh because the number names take longer to pronounce.**", "- Both show that **verbal memory depends on speech sounds**, one through confusions and the other through pronunciation time."]}, {"id": 160, "a": ["A **problem** involves an **obstacle between a present state and a goal**, with a **solution that is not immediately obvious.**", "- The obstacle is what **prevents an immediate move** from where the person is to where they want to be.", "- If the solution were obvious, there would be no problem to solve."]}, {"id": 161, "a": ["The **present state** describes **the situation as it currently exists.** The **goal** describes **the situation that the person is trying to reach.**", "- The **obstacle prevents an immediate move from one to the other.**", "- In the numbered-tile puzzle, the present state is the starting arrangement of the tiles, and the goal is the desired arrangement."]}, {"id": 162, "a": ["**Knowing the desired arrangement does not supply the sequence that produces it.** A person can identify **exactly what counts as a solution** while still having a problem, because **the route from the current arrangement to that solution is unclear.**", "- In the tile puzzle, reaching the goal **requires changing the positions of the tiles** and not merely recognizing the numbers.", "- The distinction concerns **the goal and the means of reaching it**, and not whether the goal has been stated."]}, {"id": 163, "a": ["**Gestalt psychologists** took a **perceptual approach** to **learning, problem solving, and attitudes and beliefs** as well as perception, and **Koffka (1935)** is associated with this broader approach.", "- **Problem solving involves representing a problem in the mind**, so the **organization a person gives the problem can affect the solution that becomes available.**", "- The main theme is therefore that **how a problem is represented and perceived shapes how it can be solved.**"]}, {"id": 164, "a": ["**Restructuring** means **reorganizing the problem’s representation.**", "- **Different people can represent the same physical arrangement differently**, and the representation can emphasize relationships that are **not immediately obvious** from the way the problem was first encountered.", "- **Changing that organization can make a previously hidden relationship usable.**"]}, {"id": 165, "a": ["In **Köhler’s circle problem**, the task is to **find the length of line x when the circle’s radius has length r.** **The black line is one diagonal of a rectangle whose lower-right corner is at the circle’s center**, and **the dashed line from the upper-left corner on the circle to that center is a radius.**", "- **The two diagonals of a rectangle have equal length**, so the **black diagonal equals the dashed radius, giving x = r.**", "- The useful change is **recognizing the rectangle and its equal diagonals** rather than treating the line as an isolated unknown inside a complicated circle."]}, {"id": 166, "a": ["**Insight** is **the sudden realization of a problem’s solution.**", "- A person can first perceive an object or problem **in one way and then represent it differently.**", "- **Discovering a crucial element within that changed representation** can make the solution available all at once."]}, {"id": 167, "a": ["Gestalt psychologists connected reorganization with the **Aha experience.** The experience concerns **how the solution becomes available to the solver.**", "- Instead of feeling **a series of increasingly complete steps**, the person experiences **a sudden change from not having the solution to recognizing it.**"]}, {"id": 168, "a": ["No. **Problem solving also includes solutions reached without an Aha experience.**", "- **Some problems permit a more methodical process** in which **successive operations bring the person closer to an answer.**", "- **Sudden realization describes insight**, but it does not describe every way that a problem can be solved."]}, {"id": 169, "a": ["**Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)** compared **insight problems**, including the **triangle and chain problems**, with **noninsight problems**, including **algebra.**", "- Participants attempted the problems and **repeatedly judged how close they felt to a solution.**", "- The comparison concerned **the course of solving**, and not only whether a final answer was correct."]}, {"id": 170, "a": ["In **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)**, for **insight problems**, the prediction was that participants would **not be very good at anticipating how near they were to a solution**, because **a crucial change in representation could make the answer available without a steadily increasing feeling of progress.**", "- For **noninsight problems**, the process was predicted to be more **methodical**. **Completing successive algebraic operations could give the solver information about progress**, so **perceived closeness should increase as the solution approaches.**", "- The two problem types therefore produced **different predictions about awareness during solving.**"]}, {"id": 171, "a": ["In **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)**, participants made **warmth judgments every 15 seconds.**", "- **Hot ratings meant feeling close to the solution**, and **colder ratings meant feeling farther away.**", "- The reports gave **a repeated measure of subjective progress** that could be compared with when the participant actually solved the problem."]}, {"id": 172, "a": ["The **triangle problem** begins with **ten blue dots arranged in four rows** so the triangle points upward. The task is to **move exactly three dots** and make the triangle **point toward the bottom of the page.**", "- The **original top dot can become a new bottom point beneath the center of the original bottom row.**", "- **The two outer dots of the original bottom row move to the outer ends of the original two-dot row.** That row then has four dots, with three, two and one below it, producing a **downward-pointing triangle.**"]}, {"id": 173, "a": ["The **chain problem** provides **four short sections of chain** that must become **one closed loop.** **Opening a link costs 2 cents, closing it costs 3 cents, and the budget is 15 cents**, so the budget permits **three** opening-and-closing operations.", "- **One three-link section can be opened into three separate links.** Those links **connect the remaining three sections into a closed loop**, with each opened link closed once.", "- Treating **one whole section as connecting material** changes the organization of the problem and uses the full 15-cent budget."]}, {"id": 174, "a": ["The task is to **move exactly three dots**, and **seven dots must remain in place.**", "- **Relocating the entire arrangement would violate the task.**", "- The solution therefore has to come from moving a few specific dots, which is part of what makes the change of representation necessary."]}, {"id": 175, "a": ["In **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)**, ratings for **algebra** increased **gradually** as the solution approached. The feeling of getting closer **developed over a period of time.**", "- For **insight problems**, **warmth remained relatively low until close to solution and then increased sharply.**", "- The **large jump from about 3 to 7** contrasts with the more gradual algebra pattern."]}, {"id": 176, "a": ["In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), the ratings concern **subjective closeness to a solution.** **A low rating shortly before an insight solution means that the person did not yet feel close, even though the answer was imminent.**", "- The contrast connects **sudden insight with a different experience of progress** from methodical noninsight solving.", "- It does not make all successful problem solving equally sudden."]}, {"id": 177, "a": ["**Fixation** is a **tendency to focus on a specific characteristic of a problem in a way that prevents a solution.** **Gestalt psychologists treated it as a major obstacle to problem solving.**", "- A **familiar interpretation can hold attention** even when another interpretation would make the available materials useful."]}, {"id": 178, "a": ["**Functional fixedness** **restricts an object to its familiar functions.**", "- The **obstacle comes from the familiar use assigned to the object** and not from an absence of useful material.", "- It is a specific kind of fixation: the object’s usual job blocks seeing another job it could do."]}, {"id": 179, "a": ["In the **candle problem**, a person has **a candle, matches and their box, and tacks**, and must **mount the candle on a vertical corkboard** so it can burn **without dripping wax onto the floor.**", "- The **matchbox can be attached to the corkboard and used as a platform for the candle.**", "- **Treating the box only as a container for matches interferes with recognizing that support function.**"]}, {"id": 180, "a": ["**Participants given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve the problem.**", "- **Separating the box from its familiar contents made it easier to treat the box as an available object with another possible use.**", "- The changed presentation affected **whether the support function became available to the solver.**"]}, {"id": 181, "a": ["The **two-string problem** requires **tying together two strings hanging from the ceiling.** They are **far enough apart that a person holding one cannot simply reach the other.**", "- **A pair of pliers can be tied to one string and used as a weight.** **Setting that string swinging creates a pendulum.**", "- The person **holds the other string and catches the weighted string when it swings close enough**, bringing the ends together."]}, {"id": 182, "a": ["**Functional fixedness** occurs when **the familiar tool function of the pliers prevents their use as a weight.**", "- **The useful property is their weight rather than their ability to grip something.**", "- **Reorganizing the object’s role** makes the string move in a way that **overcomes the original reach problem.**"]}, {"id": 183, "a": ["After the hint, **most participants were able to solve the problem.** **The moving string made its potential use as a pendulum available**, allowing the person to bring a previously unreachable string toward the other hand.", "- In Gestalt terms, **the solution involves restructuring the representation.** **The hanging string changes from something that must be reached where it is to something that can be made to swing within reach.**"]}, {"id": 184, "a": ["A **mental set** is **a preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on past experience with that problem or similar problems.**", "- The expectation concerns **the method of solving.**", "- **A person can continue applying a familiar procedure even when the current problem permits another approach.**"]}, {"id": 185, "a": ["The **water-jug problem** asks for **a required amount of water using three empty jugs with specified capacities.**", "- Each problem specifies capacities for jugs **A, B and C and a desired quantity.**", "- **Previous success with one measuring procedure can establish a mental set** that affects which operations the solver considers when the capacities change."]}, {"id": 186, "a": ["In the first problem, **A holds 21 quarts, B holds 127, C holds 3, and the goal is 100.** **Filling B and removing one A-sized amount leaves 106**, **removing one C-sized amount leaves 103**, and **removing a second C-sized amount leaves 100.**", "- The procedure is **B − A − 2C.**", "- **Repeated success with this procedure across the early problems encourages the person to treat it as the expected method.** The rule becomes a mental set because the solver brings that method to a new set of capacities."]}, {"id": 187, "a": ["In **problem 7**, **A holds 15 quarts, B holds 39, and C holds 3, with a goal of 18.** The familiar method works because **39 − 15 − 6 gives 18**, but **A + C gives 18 more directly.**", "- In **problem 8**, **A holds 28, B holds 59, and C holds 3, with a goal of 25.** **A − C gives 25 directly**, although **B − A − 2C also works.**", "- **A mental set can therefore inhibit a simpler solution even when the familiar solution remains possible.**"]}, {"id": 188, "a": ["**Newell and Simon (1956)** developed the **Logic Theorist** and described **problem solving as a process involving search.**", "- **Solving includes the activity between the posing of a problem and its solution.**", "- **The person searches for a way to reach a goal while working around obstacles.**"]}, {"id": 189, "a": ["A **problem space** includes an **initial state, the intermediate states, and a goal state.**", "- The **initial state** contains the starting conditions, **intermediate states** are the situations reached along the way, and the **goal state** meets the conditions for a solution.", "- **Knowing the initial and goal states does not specify which intermediate states will connect them.** Solving requires finding a sequence that reaches the goal."]}, {"id": 190, "a": ["**Operators** are **rules specifying which moves are allowed and which are not.** **Applying an operator changes the problem from one state to another.**", "- The rules therefore determine **which transitions can belong to a possible solution**, even when another movement would physically be possible."]}, {"id": 191, "a": ["In the **Tower of Hanoi**, disks begin **stacked on one peg** and must be **transferred to the goal peg.** **A move transfers one disk at a time.**", "- **A disk can be moved only when no other disk is on top of it**, so a buried disk is unavailable until the disks above it have been moved.", "- **A larger disk cannot be placed on top of a smaller disk.** That restriction also applies to **intermediate arrangements**, and not only to the final stack."]}, {"id": 192, "a": ["**The starting stack is the initial state**, **the completed stack on the target peg is the goal state**, and **temporary distributions across the pegs are intermediate states.**", "- **A useful route consists of legal transitions through those intermediate states**, with the operators constraining every move along the route."]}, {"id": 193, "a": ["**Transferring a Tower of Hanoi stack requires moving smaller disks out of the way before a larger disk becomes available.** The smaller disks can occupy **temporary positions on the other pegs**, but their size ordering still has to obey the rule.", "- **Moving the largest disk to the goal peg does not complete the puzzle if smaller disks remain elsewhere.** Those disks must then be **rearranged onto the larger disk in the required order.**", "- The route therefore includes **repeated transfers of smaller stacks as part of transferring the whole stack.**"]}, {"id": 194, "a": ["The **64-disk version requires roughly a trillion years even if every correct move took only one second.**", "- **Increasing the number of disks creates a very large number of required moves.**", "- **Executing each move quickly does not remove the many intermediate rearrangements imposed by the rules.**"]}, {"id": 195, "a": ["The **numbered-tile puzzle has eight tiles and one empty location in a three-by-three board.**", "- In the **initial arrangement**, the rows are **8–empty–6, 5–4–7, and 2–3–1.**", "- In the **goal arrangement**, the rows are **empty–1–2, 3–4–5, and 6–7–8.**"]}, {"id": 196, "a": ["**A tile being present on the board does not mean that its required position has been achieved.**", "- The **initial state specifies the complete starting arrangement**, and the **goal state specifies the desired arrangement of those same tiles and the empty location.**", "- **The current state has to be related to the goal through an allowable sequence of moves.**"]}, {"id": 197, "a": ["A **search tree** represents **possible states of a tile puzzle and the moves connecting them.**", "- **Continuing the search produces further branches because each intermediate arrangement can allow more than one move.**", "- **A sequence of connected states supplies a route to the goal**, while the other branches are alternatives that the search could consider. **Searching involves choosing which available states to pursue.**"]}, {"id": 198, "a": ["From the start **2–8–3, 1–6–4, 7–empty–5**, **the empty location can shift left, up or right by exchanging position with the adjacent tile.**", "- **Each resulting board is a different intermediate state**, even though all states contain **the same eight numbered tiles.**", "- One state can permit several successors under the same operator rules, which is why **a state and a move are different things.**"]}, {"id": 199, "a": ["**The numbered labels identify positions in the search**, while **the small boards specify the actual tile arrangements.**", "- **Searching involves choosing which available states to pursue.**", "- **The distinction between a state and a move matters** because one state can permit several different successors."]}, {"id": 200, "a": ["According to Newell and Simon (1956), solving requires **searching the problem space for a route to the goal.** **Means–end analysis organizes that search around reducing the difference between the current situation and the goal.**", "- The solver considers **which changes would help remove an obstacle to the desired result.**"]}, {"id": 201, "a": ["A **subgoal** is **a condition that makes part of the larger task possible**, and not the complete final answer.", "- **Achieving it can reduce an obstacle that prevents a required move**, allowing the search to proceed toward the overall goal.", "- In the Tower of Hanoi, **freeing the large disk is a useful subgoal**, even though a temporary arrangement is not the final stack."]}, {"id": 202, "a": ["The first subgoal is **freeing the large disk.** **Moving the smallest disk to the third peg and the next disk to the middle peg removes the disks resting on the largest one.**", "- The second subgoal is **freeing the third peg**, because a smaller disk there would block the move. **Moving the smallest disk from the third peg onto the smaller stack on the middle peg achieves it.**", "- The third subgoal is **moving the large disk onto the third peg**, which can now be done **without breaking the size rule.** The smaller disks still have to reach it."]}, {"id": 203, "a": ["**The subgoals address different obstacles to the same move.** **One removes the material above the disk that must move**, while **another removes an obstruction at its destination.**", "- **Means–end analysis can therefore require several coordinated intermediate changes before an action toward the overall goal becomes legal.**"]}, {"id": 204, "a": ["**How a problem is stated can affect its difficulty.** **The wording and presentation influence which relationships become part of the person’s representation.**", "- **Two presentations can preserve the same underlying task** while making **a useful property easier or harder to recognize.**", "- The relationships represented can determine whether the task is approached as **trial-and-error placement** or as **a question about a constraint on all possible placements.**"]}, {"id": 205, "a": ["The **mutilated-checkerboard problem** concerns **covering a board with dominoes after squares have been removed.**", "- An ordinary checkerboard has **64 squares and can be covered with 32 dominoes**, each covering **two adjacent squares.**", "- The problem **removes two opposite corner squares** and asks whether **31 dominoes** can cover the remaining board."]}, {"id": 206, "a": ["**Newell and Simon’s approach provides a way to specify possible pathways from an initial state to a goal state.**", "- **Identifying states and possible transitions makes the search explicit.**", "- **A solution requires an allowable route between the beginning and the required endpoint.**"]}, {"id": 207, "a": ["**Removing two squares leaves 62 squares, matching the area covered by 31 dominoes.** But **equal total area does not guarantee that the dominoes can be arranged legally.**", "- **Every domino covers adjacent squares of different colors**, so every placement uses **one square of each color.** **The two removed opposite corners have the same color**, so the remaining board has fewer squares of that color.", "- A covering would need **equal numbers of the two colors**, so **the board cannot be completely covered by 31 dominoes.** The obstacle is **the imbalance between square colors** and not insufficient total area."]}, {"id": 208, "a": ["No. **Equal total area does not guarantee that the dominoes can be arranged legally.**", "- The **62 remaining squares match the area of 31 dominoes**, but **every domino must cover one square of each color.**", "- **Representing the color relationship supplies a reason that applies to every possible arrangement**, so individual unsuccessful placement attempts are not required to establish the answer."]}, {"id": 209, "a": ["The conditions varied the information supplied about the squares. **Participants received a blank board, a colored board, a board labeled with the words black and pink, or one labeled bread and butter.**", "- **The underlying covering problem remained a pairing problem across two kinds of square.**"]}, {"id": 210, "a": ["**Participants solved the bread-and-butter condition twice as fast as the blank condition.**", "- **The bread-and-butter condition emphasized the difference between the square types and their pairing.** The labels made it easier to represent **a domino as requiring one of each kind.**", "- **Information that pointed toward the correct representation reduced solving time**, while the need to cover adjacent pairs stayed the same."]}, {"id": 211, "a": ["A **think-aloud protocol** asks a person to **say aloud what they are thinking while solving a problem.**", "- Participants are instructed to **verbalize new thoughts** and not simply describe the actions they are performing.", "- The record concerns **the information receiving attention during the attempt.**"]}, {"id": 212, "a": ["In the **bread-and-butter version**, participants can reach the solution after **recognizing that the words bread and butter are relevant to the required pairing.**", "- Their thinking shifts from **considering squares only as positions** to **considering the relationship between the two kinds of square.**", "- That change is a **representational shift related to the Gestalt account of restructuring.** **Verbal reports can identify when the pairing of the two labels enters the person’s reasoning.**"]}, {"id": 213, "a": ["The **Gestalt approach** emphasizes **how a problem is represented and restructured**, with **insight** as **a sudden realization.**", "- **Newell and Simon (1956)** describe **solving as searching a problem space** of states, using **operators** to move between them.", "- One approach asks how the person organizes the problem, and the other asks how a route through the possible states is found."]}, {"id": 214, "a": ["In **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)**, **algebra warmth rose gradually.** **Insight warmth stayed low and then jumped sharply.**", "- Insight therefore **does not give a steady feeling of getting closer.**", "- Methodical solving does, because successive operations provide information about progress."]}, {"id": 215, "a": ["**Restructuring is the change in representation**, and **insight is the sudden realization that follows** once a crucial element is found in the new representation.", "- Restructuring describes what changes in how the problem is organized, and insight describes how the solution then feels to the solver."]}, {"id": 216, "a": ["**Functional fixedness** **restricts an object to its familiar function.** A **mental set** is **a preconceived approach to solving based on past experience.**", "- **Both are obstacles to solving**, but **one concerns objects and the other concerns methods.**"]}, {"id": 217, "a": ["In the **candle problem** and the **two-string problem**, **an everyday object has a familiar function that blocks seeing its useful property.**", "- The **matchbox’s familiar job is holding matches**, which blocks seeing it as a **platform.**", "- The **pliers’ familiar job is gripping**, which blocks seeing them as a **weight.**"]}, {"id": 218, "a": ["**The hanging string changes from something to be reached where it is into something that can be made to swing within reach**, so **the representation changes.**", "- The hint made the **string’s potential use as a pendulum available**, which is how restructuring is described."]}, {"id": 219, "a": ["**Participants given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve the candle problem**, and **participants given bread-and-butter labels solved the checkerboard twice as fast as the blank condition.**", "- In each case, **the presentation made a useful relationship easier to represent.**"]}, {"id": 220, "a": ["**Repeated success with B − A − 2C builds a mental set.**", "- **The simpler A + C or A − C solutions can be missed even though the familiar method still works.**", "- **A mental set can inhibit a simpler solution** because the method that experience makes available comes first."]}, {"id": 221, "a": ["**Operators define legal moves that change one state into another.**", "- **Search is finding a sequence of those moves from the initial state through intermediate states to the goal.**", "- Together they describe a problem space: the states, the rules for moving between them, and the route found by searching."]}, {"id": 222, "a": ["Each has an **initial state, a goal state, intermediate states and rules for legal moves**, and **solving requires choosing a route through those states.**", "- In the **Tower of Hanoi**, the rules limit which disks can move.", "- In the **tile puzzle**, the empty location can exchange with an adjacent tile."]}, {"id": 223, "a": ["**A subgoal makes a later required move legal**, such as **freeing the large disk**, even though **the temporary arrangement is not the goal.**", "- It is **a condition that makes part of the larger task possible**, and not the complete final answer."]}, {"id": 224, "a": ["**Means–end analysis organizes search around reducing the difference between the current state and the goal**, using subgoals.", "- It is a way of carrying out the search through a problem space that **Newell and Simon** described."]}, {"id": 225, "a": ["**Problem statement** and the **Gestalt idea of representation** both say that **the way a problem is represented changes which relationships are noticed.**", "- **Wording and labels** influence the representation, and so they change **how hard the problem is.**", "- Restructuring is what happens when the representation changes in a way that makes the useful relationship available."]}, {"id": 226, "a": ["**The color pairing of every domino and the same-color removed corners show impossibility without trying individual placements.**", "- The constraint **applies to every possible arrangement**, so no single unsuccessful attempt is needed to establish that the covering cannot be done."]}, {"id": 227, "a": ["The **bread-and-butter labels pointed toward the pairing of two kinds of square**, which **changed how squares were represented.**", "- That matches the **Gestalt idea that representation shifts make the solution available.**", "- The objects and the covering requirement stayed the same."]}, {"id": 228, "a": ["**Verbal reports show when the pairing of the two labels entered the person’s reasoning.**", "- The shift from **squares as positions** to **a relationship between two kinds of square** can be observed **during the attempt**, so the representational shift becomes visible."]}, {"id": 229, "a": ["The **triangle and chain problems are solved by a change in representation**, such as using **a whole section as connecting links.**", "- In **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)** they were compared with **algebra, which has a more methodical route.**", "- **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)** classed them as **insight problems.**"]}, {"id": 230, "a": ["**Fixation, functional fixedness and mental set are obstacles that arise from how the problem is represented or approached.**", "- The **64-disk Tower of Hanoi** shows that **the number of required moves can be very large**, which is a property of the search.", "- One kind of difficulty comes from the person’s representation, and the other comes from the size of the route."]}, {"id": 231, "a": ["Each problem has **an obstacle between a present state and a goal**, and **the solution is not immediately obvious.**", "- The solution is found by **restructuring the representation** or by **searching for a route** through states."]}, {"id": 232, "a": ["**Insight is a sudden realization after restructuring**, and in **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)** **warmth for insight problems stayed low and then jumped sharply.**", "- **Noninsight problems such as algebra allow a more methodical process**, and **warmth rose gradually.**", "- The difference is in how the solution is reached and how close the solver feels along the way."]}, {"id": 233, "a": ["**Reorganization and insight concern how a problem is represented in the mind.** A person can initially organize the elements in a way that **hides a useful relationship.**", "- **Reorganizing that representation can make a solution available**, sometimes with the **sudden realization associated with insight.**"]}, {"id": 234, "a": ["**Problem solving as search** concerns **the activity between posing a problem and reaching its solution.** **The solver has to find a route through possible states.**", "- **Specifying the starting conditions and the goal does not by itself identify the sequence that connects them.**"]}, {"id": 235, "a": ["**How a problem is stated can affect its difficulty** because **the presentation influences the relationships a person represents.**", "- In the **mutilated-checkerboard problem**, the board and dominoes can be considered through their **total area** or through **the pairing of different-colored squares.**", "- **The color relationship makes a constraint available that the area calculation alone does not supply.**"]}, {"id": 236, "a": ["The **Russian marriage problem** begins with **32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women.** A matchmaker arranges **32 heterosexual marriages**, pairing one person from each group.", "- **Two bachelors then die after a contest involving pierogies.** There are **62 survivors, consisting of 30 men and 32 women.**", "- The question asks whether rearranging the surviving people can produce **31 heterosexual marriages** under the same one-person-from-each-group requirement."]}, {"id": 237, "a": ["The answer is **no because the two groups no longer have equal numbers.** **At most 30 of the required marriages can be formed, leaving two women without a partner from the other group.**", "- **Dividing 62 people by two gives 31 possible pairs by total size**, but it does not satisfy the requirement about **which kinds of person each pair must contain.**", "- **The obstacle remains even if every existing marriage is rearranged.** **No rearrangement creates an additional member of the smaller group.** Solving depends on **recognizing the imbalance between the two categories** rather than searching through possible matches one at a time."]}, {"id": 238, "a": ["**A domino covers two adjacent checkerboard squares, one of each color.** **Removing two opposite corner squares removes two squares of the same color.**", "- The remaining board **contains unequal numbers of the two colors**, even though its **62 squares have the same total area as 31 dominoes.**", "- The **two square colors play the roles of the two kinds of member needed for each pair.** **Total area gives the number of pairs that would be needed**, while **the color counts determine whether those particular pairs can exist.**"]}, {"id": 239, "a": ["**Every proposed complete covering would have to pair each square of one color with a square of the other color.** **Because one color is more numerous, some squares would remain unmatched.**", "- **Moving the dominoes into a different arrangement cannot change that imbalance.**", "- **The impossibility follows from the pairing requirement shared by all legal domino placements.**"]}, {"id": 240, "a": ["**Analogical problem solving** **uses the solution to a similar problem to guide the solution to a new problem.** Instead of working the new problem out from scratch, the person draws on how a similar problem was solved.", "- **The target problem is the problem currently being solved**, the one that needs an answer.", "- **A source problem is another problem that shares relevant similarities with it**, and its solution is what is carried over."]}, {"id": 241, "a": ["The **Russian marriage problem can serve as the source and the mutilated-checkerboard problem as the target.**", "- **Marriages correspond to dominoes, and the two groups of people correspond to the two square colors.** **A valid marriage needs one member of each group, just as a domino placement needs one square of each color.**", "- **Removing two bachelors corresponds to removing two squares of the same color.** **Both changes leave an imbalance between the categories that must be paired.**"]}, {"id": 242, "a": ["**Analogical transfer** is **the transfer from the source problem to the target problem.**", "- **Learning about the Russian marriage problem improves the ability to solve the checkerboard problem** because the source supplies a way to **recognize the required one-of-each pairing.**", "- **The useful information is the relationship among the elements**, rather than their identical appearance."]}, {"id": 243, "a": ["**Duncker’s radiation problem** concerns a **patient with a malignant tumor in the stomach.** **An operation is impossible**, and the **tumor must be destroyed for the patient to survive.**", "- **A ray can destroy the tumor if sufficiently high intensity reaches it at once.**"]}, {"id": 244, "a": ["**A high-intensity ray would also destroy the healthy tissue that it passes through on the way to the tumor.**", "- **A low-intensity ray would spare that healthy tissue but would not destroy the tumor.**", "- **Choosing a single high or low intensity therefore fails one of the two requirements.**"]}, {"id": 245, "a": ["**Several low-intensity rays can converge on the tumor from different directions at the same time.** **Each path through the surrounding tissue receives a low intensity, while the combined intensity at the tumor is sufficient to destroy it.**", "- **The strong effect is concentrated at the target rather than delivered through one surrounding path.**", "- **Only about 10% of participants arrive at the correct solution without the helpful analogy.**"]}, {"id": 246, "a": ["In the **fortress problem**, a **dictator rules from a strong fortress in the middle of a country**, and a **rebel general has an army strong enough to capture the fortress if the entire force attacks it.**", "- **Mines on each road allow small groups to pass but detonate when a large force travels together.**", "- **Sending the entire army down one road would destroy the road and damage neighboring villages**, and **a small group can travel safely but lacks the force required to capture the fortress.**"]}, {"id": 247, "a": ["**The general can divide the army into small groups approaching along different roads.** **Each group remains below the danger threshold on its route.**", "- **If the groups arrive together, their combined force at the fortress is sufficient for the attack.**", "- The roads impose a limit on what can pass safely, and the fortress imposes a requirement on the total force that must arrive. **Coordinating separate groups satisfies those two constraints together.**"]}, {"id": 248, "a": ["**The fortress corresponds to the tumor, the small groups correspond to low-intensity rays, and the roads correspond to paths through surrounding tissue.**", "- **Sending one large force down one road corresponds to sending one damaging high-intensity ray through healthy tissue.**", "- In both solutions, **convergence preserves a strong effect at the destination while limiting what travels through each approach.**"]}, {"id": 249, "a": ["**Timing is part of the relationship**, because **the separate contributions must combine at the target.** In the fortress problem the small groups have to arrive together, and in the radiation problem the low-intensity rays have to meet at the tumor.", "- **Merely dividing the army, or lowering every ray’s intensity, without combining the effects would leave the target requirement unmet.** A small group alone cannot capture the fortress, and a low-intensity ray alone cannot destroy the tumor.", "- The solution works because the weak approaches add up at the target while staying weak along each road or path."]}, {"id": 250, "a": ["**Gick and Holyoak** distinguished three steps. **Noticing** is **recognizing that a source problem may be relevant to the current target**, and it is **crucial and can be the most difficult step.**", "- **Mapping** **connects the elements and relationships of the source to those of the target.**", "- **Applying the mapping** **uses those correspondences to generate a parallel solution for the target.**"]}, {"id": 251, "a": ["After reading the fortress story, **about 30% of participants solved the radiation problem.**", "- **Many participants had encountered the source without recognizing how it applied to the medical target.**", "- **Availability of the story in memory therefore did not guarantee spontaneous use of its solution.**"]}, {"id": 252, "a": ["**When participants were told to think about the story they had read, success rose to about 75%.**", "- **The reminder helped make a relevant source available for mapping and application.**", "- The difference separates **encountering a useful analogy from noticing and using the relationship** when solving another problem."]}, {"id": 253, "a": ["**Surface features** are **the specific elements of a problem**, such as **a fortress, an army, a tumor, or a ray.** They are the objects and details that describe the situation.", "- **Those details help describe the situation**, but **two problems can use very different objects while sharing the relationship needed for a solution.**", "- They can therefore be a poor guide to which problems are really alike."]}, {"id": 254, "a": ["**A fortress and a tumor do not look alike and belong to different kinds of situation.** **Focusing on that difference can keep a person from noticing that both tasks require a strong combined effect at a central target while keeping the separate approaches safe.**", "- **Making surface features more similar can make an analogous relationship easier to notice.**", "- **Noticing concerns finding a potentially relevant source, while mapping concerns working out how its elements and relationships correspond to the target.**"]}, {"id": 255, "a": ["In the **lightbulb problem**, **Ruth finds an expensive laboratory lightbulb with a broken filament.** **The bulb must produce precisely controlled light, but its surrounding glass is sealed**, so she cannot open it to repair the filament directly.", "- The task is to **fuse the two broken parts while leaving the surrounding glass intact.**", "- **A brief, high-intensity laser beam could fuse the filament, but it would also break the fragile glass**, and **a low-intensity beam would preserve the glass but would not fuse the filament.**"]}, {"id": 256, "a": ["**Several low-intensity laser beams can be directed toward the filament from different directions at once.** **Their combined effect at the filament supplies enough intensity to fuse it**, while **each part of the surrounding glass receives only a low-intensity beam.**", "- **The filament needs a strong local effect, while the glass limits the intensity that can pass safely along an individual path.**"]}, {"id": 257, "a": ["**Duncker’s radiation problem can act as a source for the lightbulb problem.** **The tumor and broken filament are targets requiring a strong local effect**, and **healthy tissue and fragile glass are surroundings that a single high-intensity approach would damage.**", "- **About 81% of participants who knew the radiation problem solved the lightbulb problem**, compared with **about 10% in the control group.**", "- **Surface similarity can help make a useful analogy available**, allowing its converging-beam solution to be applied."]}, {"id": 258, "a": ["**The two tasks share relatively similar surface features.** **Both involve directing a beam toward an internal target.**", "- **That similarity makes it easier to notice a possible connection than when comparing medical radiation with an army approaching a fortress.**"]}, {"id": 259, "a": ["**Structural features** are **the underlying principle that governs the solution.**", "- **In the radiation problem, a strong ray destroys healthy tissue on its way to the tumor.** **In the fragile-glass lightbulb problem, a strong laser breaks the glass on its way to the filament.**", "- **Both problems require enough intensity at an internal target while restricting the intensity delivered through its surroundings.** **That relationship explains why several low-intensity approaches must converge.**"]}, {"id": 260, "a": ["**Two versions of the lightbulb problem can keep the same surface features while changing the structural features.** **Using the same bulb, filament and lasers holds the object descriptions similar.**", "- **Changing why a single laser fails changes the relationship between the source and the radiation target.**"]}, {"id": 261, "a": ["In the **fragile-glass source problem**, **a high-intensity beam would fuse the broken filament but would also break the surrounding glass**, and **lower intensity would protect the glass but fail to repair the filament.**", "- **Ruth places several lasers around the lightbulb and directs their low-intensity beams toward the filament at the same time.**", "- **The radiation target has the same relevant structure**: **healthy tissue limits the strength of an individual incoming ray, but the tumor requires a stronger combined intensity.**"]}, {"id": 262, "a": ["**About 69% of participants who read the fragile-glass solution solved the radiation problem.**", "- **The source supplies both a converging-beam procedure and the constraint that explains why that procedure is needed.**", "- **The useful correspondence concerns the relationship between a strong target effect and protected surroundings.**"]}, {"id": 263, "a": ["In the **insufficient-intensity source problem**, **the laser generates only low-intensity beams that cannot fuse the filament.** **The obstacle is an inadequate intensity from the available source.**", "- **Ruth again places several lasers around the bulb and directs their beams toward the filament together**, and **their combined effect supplies the intensity needed to fuse it.**", "- **This version does not make damage to surrounding fragile glass the reason that a single high-intensity beam cannot be used.**"]}, {"id": 264, "a": ["**About 33% of participants who read the insufficient-intensity solution solved the radiation problem**, compared with **about 69% after the fragile-glass source.**", "- **Both source solutions use converging laser beams**, but they give different reasons for combining the beams.", "- **The different transfer rates accompany a difference in the underlying constraints.** The radiation problem requires both **sufficient effect at the tumor and protection of the surrounding healthy tissue.**"]}, {"id": 265, "a": ["**Analogical encoding** is **the process of comparing two problems and determining the similarities between them.**", "- **Comparing the problems encourages attention to relationships that appear in both**, rather than considering each set of surface details in isolation."]}, {"id": 266, "a": ["**Structural features are the underlying principles that govern a problem’s solution**, and **making these relationships more apparent can improve analogical problem solving.**", "- **Having participants compare source stories can direct their reasoning toward the structural features that govern the solution.**", "- **A later target can then be approached through the common relationship identified across the sources**, instead of depending entirely on the appearance of one familiar object or situation."]}, {"id": 267, "a": ["The **analogical paradox** is **the contrast between difficulty applying analogies in laboratory tasks and the routine use of analogies in real-world settings.**", "- **A person can fail to recognize an experimentally supplied source yet use relationships from other situations during ordinary problem solving.**"]}, {"id": 268, "a": ["**Scientists use about 3–15 analogies in a one-hour laboratory meeting.**", "- **Their discussion can involve connecting a current question with another situation or known solution.**", "- **Analogical reasoning therefore occurs within ongoing work**, rather than only when an experiment explicitly assigns a source and target problem."]}, {"id": 269, "a": ["**In vivo problem-solving research observes people solving problems in real-world settings.**", "- **The naturalistic setting retains the context in which the activity ordinarily occurs**, including the information and interactions that are part of that work.", "- **These observations can be time-consuming, and researchers cannot isolate and control variables as they can in a more constrained experiment.**"]}, {"id": 270, "a": ["An **expert** is **a person recognized as extremely knowledgeable or skilled in a particular field after devoting a large amount of time to learning, practicing, and applying that learning.**", "- The definition connects expertise to **substantial experience within a domain**, rather than simply confidence about a task."]}, {"id": 271, "a": ["**A chess expert can have spent approximately 10,000–20,000 hours studying and playing chess.**", "- **Experts solve problems within their field faster and with a higher success rate than beginners.**", "- The comparison is performance **in the area in which their knowledge and skill have developed.**"]}, {"id": 272, "a": ["**Chase and Simon (1973)** compared **a chess master with a beginner who had fewer than 100 hours of chess experience.**", "- **Each person viewed a chessboard arrangement for five seconds** and then **attempted to reproduce where the pieces had been.**", "- **One condition used actual game positions, while the other used random arrangements.**"]}, {"id": 273, "a": ["In Chase and Simon (1973), **the expert performed much better than the beginner for actual game positions.**", "- **Long-term memory made meaningful arrangements available as chunks**, and **a chunk groups related information into a useful unit.**", "- **The same familiar chunks were not available for the random condition.** **The contrast connects the expert’s strong recall to organized domain knowledge.**"]}, {"id": 274, "a": ["**Knowledge organization affects how information can be accessed when solving a problem.** What matters is not only how much a person knows but how that knowledge is arranged.", "- **Knowing more facts is accompanied by differences in how relevant problems are grouped and related to the principles needed to solve them.**", "- The experts in **Chi et al. (1982)** grouped problems by principle, which is an example of knowledge organized around what is needed to solve problems."]}, {"id": 275, "a": ["**Chi et al. (1982)** presented **24 physics problems** to **experts who were physics professors** and **novices who were students with one semester of physics.**", "- **Participants sorted the problems into groups according to their similarities.**", "- The task concerned **how they represented relationships among problems**, rather than merely whether they could recite a definition."]}, {"id": 276, "a": ["In Chi et al. (1982), **novices grouped problems by surface features, such as the appearance of the objects involved.**", "- **Experts grouped problems by structural features, including the underlying principles involved.**", "- **The organization makes knowledge accessible through a relationship relevant to solving**, rather than only through a matching object description."]}, {"id": 277, "a": ["In Chi et al. (1982), **the novice grouped problems 23 and 24 together because both involve inclined planes.**", "- **The expert grouped problems 21 and 24 together through conservation of energy.** **Problem 21 contains a block and spring on a horizontal surface**, while **problem 24 contains blocks on an incline.**", "- **The same problem 24 therefore belongs with a different partner under the two organizations.**"]}, {"id": 278, "a": ["**Experts spend more time analyzing a problem before proceeding with a solution.**", "- **Their initial approach can be slower because they examine the problem and identify relevant relationships.**", "- **That investment can pay off in solving the overall problem efficiently.**"]}, {"id": 279, "a": ["No. **The advantage is specific to the expert’s field.** **Experts are no better than novices when given problems outside that field.**", "- **The knowledge and organization developed through practice do not automatically supply the relationships required by an unrelated problem.**"]}, {"id": 280, "a": ["**Expertise can also make a person less open to new ways of looking at a problem.**", "- **A highly familiar approach can remain the preferred interpretation even when the task calls for another one.**", "- **Some problems require rejecting usual procedures in favor of less frequently used procedures**, and relying on an established approach can then become a disadvantage."]}, {"id": 281, "a": ["**Creativity** includes **innovative thinking, novel ideas, and new connections between existing ideas.**", "- **Divergent thinking is open-ended and allows a large number of potential solutions.**", "- The activity concerns **generating possibilities** rather than being confined to a single immediately available response."]}, {"id": 282, "a": ["**Creative problem solving usually involves more than obtaining an idea.**", "- **It can include a lengthy period of trial and error as possibilities are developed and tested.**", "- **An idea becomes part of a process that also determines what the problem is and how a proposed response can be carried out.**"]}, {"id": 283, "a": ["Creative problem solving has **four parts**, and they run from finding the problem to acting on a solution. **Problem generation** includes **problem finding and fact finding**, so it establishes what the problem is.", "- **Problem formulation** includes **defining the problem and finding ideas**, which develops possible approaches.", "- **Problem solving** includes **evaluation and planning**, so ideas are assessed and a response is planned.", "- **Solution implementation** means **taking action**, which carries the solution through to an outcome."]}, {"id": 284, "a": ["**Generating ideas is one part of the creative process. Without ideas, the process stops because there is no proposed approach to develop.**", "- **Producing an idea, however, does not complete the evaluation, planning, and action involved in carrying a solution out.**"]}, {"id": 285, "a": ["**Too much knowledge can sometimes hinder creative problem solving when familiar approaches constrain which possibilities are considered.**", "- **An accepted procedure can dominate the representation even when the problem requires a different relationship or a less familiar approach.**"]}, {"id": 286, "a": ["**Thinking flexibly includes being willing to reject an accepted procedure when it does not fit the problem.**", "- **Knowledge remains available, but the solver has to reconsider how it is being used.**", "- **The difficulty concerns relying rigidly on an established way of proceeding rather than considering another possible solution.**"]}, {"id": 287, "a": ["The **Russian marriage problem is the source and the checkerboard is the target.** **Marriages map to dominoes and the two groups to the two colors.**", "- **Recognizing the one-of-each pairing transfers the solution** from one problem to the other.", "- **Removing two bachelors** corresponds to **removing two same-colored squares**, so both leave an imbalance."]}, {"id": 288, "a": ["**Noticing** finds a relevant source, **mapping** connects its elements to the target, and **applying** generates the parallel solution.", "- **Gick and Holyoak’s results show that success can fail at noticing even when the source is known.**", "- About 30 percent solved the radiation problem after the fortress story, and about 75 percent did when reminded."]}, {"id": 289, "a": ["**Both require enough force at a central target while limiting what travels along each approach.**", "- **The solution in each is combining weak approaches into a strong effect.**", "- The **fortress, small groups and roads** correspond to the **tumor, low-intensity rays and paths through tissue.**"]}, {"id": 290, "a": ["A **reminder to think about the story raised success from about 30 to about 75 percent**, so **noticing matters.**", "- **The radiation source gave about 81 percent on the lightbulb problem**, so **similar surface features also help.**", "- Together they show that transfer depends on **noticing that a source is relevant**, and that a source that looks similar is noticed more easily."]}, {"id": 291, "a": ["**Similar surface features make a connection easier to notice**, as in **radiation to lightbulb.**", "- **Structural features**, the **constraints that make convergence useful**, determine **whether the solution applies.**", "- Surface features help you find the source, and structure is what makes the source useful."]}, {"id": 292, "a": ["**About 69 percent solved the radiation problem after the fragile-glass source**, which **shares the surrounding-damage constraint** with the radiation problem: a strong beam would damage the surroundings on its way to the target.", "- **About 33 percent did after the insufficient-intensity source**, which **gives a different reason for converging beams**: the laser can only make low-intensity beams, so beams are combined to reach enough intensity, and there is no surrounding-damage constraint.", "- Both sources use converging laser beams, and **the different rates accompany a difference in the underlying constraints.**"]}, {"id": 293, "a": ["**Comparing sources draws attention to shared structure**, which helps people **notice and map relationships** that they do not spontaneously use.", "- This addresses the difficulty shown by **Gick and Holyoak**, where many participants did not apply a source they had read."]}, {"id": 294, "a": ["That contrast is the **analogical paradox.**", "- **In vivo research** studies analogies in real settings where **scientists use many in one meeting**, at the cost of **experimental control.**", "- The laboratory shows how hard noticing can be, and real work shows that analogies are common."]}, {"id": 295, "a": ["**In both, expertise organizes knowledge by meaningful relationships**, such as **chunks of piece positions in chess** and **structural principles in physics.**", "- **Chase and Simon (1973)** found the expert’s advantage for **meaningful arrangements.**", "- **Chi et al. (1982)** found experts **grouped problems by underlying principles.**"]}, {"id": 296, "a": ["**Chase and Simon (1973)** measured **memory for chess positions**, comparing meaningful game arrangements with random ones.", "- **Chi et al. (1982)** measured **how problems are grouped**, by surface features for novices and structural features for experts."]}, {"id": 297, "a": ["**In Chase and Simon (1973), the expert’s advantage comes from familiar chunks.**", "- **Randomizing the pieces removes the relationships that experience makes useful**, so the expert does not have chunks to use."]}, {"id": 298, "a": ["**Both reflect attention to surface features**, whether **similar-looking objects** for the **novices in Chi et al. (1982)** or **different-looking scenarios** in **Gick and Holyoak.**", "- **The structural principle that governs the solution is what is missed**, and that is what experts, grouping by principles, tend to use."]}, {"id": 299, "a": ["**Experts spend more time analyzing a problem before proceeding**, because they examine the problem and identify relevant relationships. Their **organized knowledge helps within the field**, so **extra analysis can pay off in solving the problem efficiently.**", "- **The advantage is specific to the expert’s field**: **experts are no better than novices when given problems outside that field.**", "- Knowledge and organization developed through practice **do not automatically supply the relationships required by an unrelated problem**, which is why the advantage does not carry over."]}, {"id": 300, "a": ["**Expertise can make a person less open to new ways of looking at a problem**, because **a highly familiar approach can remain the preferred interpretation** even when the task calls for another one.", "- **Too much knowledge can sometimes hinder creative problem solving when familiar approaches constrain which possibilities are considered**, so **an accepted procedure can dominate the representation.**", "- In both, **some problems require rejecting usual procedures in favor of less frequently used procedures**, and relying on the established approach becomes a disadvantage."]}, {"id": 301, "a": ["**Reorganization and insight concern how a problem is represented in the mind**, and a person can organize the elements in a way that hides a useful relationship. **Reorganizing the representation can make a solution available.**", "- In an **analogy**, **the relationship among the elements of a source is used to recognize the relationship in the target.** A relationship that was hard to see in the target becomes visible through the source.", "- In both cases the solution becomes available when the **relationship among the elements** is recognized, as when the Russian marriage problem’s one-of-each pairing is seen in the checkerboard."]}, {"id": 302, "a": ["**Divergent thinking is open-ended and allows a large number of potential solutions**, so it supplies the ideas. **Problem formulation includes finding ideas**, which is where this generation of possibilities fits.", "- **Evaluation and planning**, which belong to problem solving, and **solution implementation**, which means taking action, are **separate parts** that come after.", "- **Generating several ideas does not itself evaluate them, plan a response or put it into practice**, so the later parts are what carry an idea through to an outcome."]}, {"id": 303, "a": ["**Knowledge helps when it is organized around structure and hurts when a familiar approach dominates.**", "- **Flexible use and noticing are what matter**, in analogy and in creative problem solving."]}, {"id": 304, "a": ["**Problem generation includes finding the problem and facts**, **formulation defines the problem and finds ideas**, and **solving includes evaluation and planning.**", "- **Solution implementation then takes action.**", "- The earlier parts establish what needs to be addressed, and the later parts carry out a response."]}, {"id": 305, "a": ["The **Russian marriage problem** and the **mutilated checkerboard** have different objects but the same **pairing structure.** In the marriage problem, **each marriage needs one person from each of two groups**, and the groups ended up unequal, **30 men and 32 women.**", "- In the checkerboard, **each domino covers one square of each of two colors**, and removing two same-colored corners left **unequal numbers of the two colors.**", "- **One representation, a pairing of two kinds of member, explains why neither can be completed**, even though the 62 survivors and the 62 squares match the total number needed."]}, {"id": 306, "a": ["**Similar surface features make a connection easier to notice**, and **the structural features, the constraints that make a solution useful, determine whether it applies.**", "- **Transfer was higher after the fragile-glass source than after the insufficient-intensity source**, and the different transfer rates **accompany a difference in the underlying constraints.**"]}, {"id": 307, "a": ["**Decisions** are **the process of making choices between alternatives.** **Reasoning** is **the process of drawing conclusions.**", "- **A choice concerns which alternative to take**, while **a conclusion concerns what follows from the information available.**", "- **The two processes can contribute to the same activity without being identical.**"]}, {"id": 308, "a": ["**Decisions are based on the judgments people make.** Applying those judgments can involve **reasoning about the available evidence and what it means for the alternatives.**", "- **A judgment can contribute to a decision through an evidence-based inference.**", "- **The conclusion expresses what the observations support, and the decision concerns what to do among the alternatives available.**"]}, {"id": 309, "a": ["**Inductive reasoning** is **one of the primary mechanisms involved in making judgments.** It means **reasoning based on observations and reaching conclusions from evidence.**", "- **Information about what has been observed supports a conclusion about the situation**, rather than the conclusion being selected without reference to evidence."]}, {"id": 310, "a": ["**Inductive reasoning is the basis of scientific investigations.** **Observations are made, data are collected, and conclusions are drawn from that evidence.**", "- **The conclusions are probably, but not definitely, true** because the observations **support an inference rather than guarantee every case** to which it might be applied."]}, {"id": 311, "a": ["**The strength of an argument depends on the representativeness, number, and quality of the observations.**", "- **Representativeness** concerns **how well the observed cases reflect the group being described.** A conclusion about a whole group is less secure when the observed cases leave out relevant variation.", "- **More observations can provide more evidence, but their number is not the only consideration.** **Quality** concerns **how compelling the observation is for the claim being considered.**"]}, {"id": 312, "a": ["**Numerous white swans can support an expectation about swan color**, but **a single clearly observed black swan contradicts the claim that every swan is white.**", "- **One high-quality observation can carry substantial weight** because of what it establishes about that particular claim.", "- It shows how much weight one high-quality observation can carry."]}, {"id": 313, "a": ["**Scientific discoveries use inductive reasoning when observations support hypotheses and general conclusions.** The reasoning **connects particular observations with a proposed regularity.**", "- **In everyday life, people predict what will happen from observations about what has happened in the past.**", "- **Predictions and choices are especially likely to draw on experience when the situation is familiar.**"]}, {"id": 314, "a": ["No. **Some inductive reasoning happens automatically, without a person deliberately listing observations and constructing an argument.**", "- **Experience can guide a response rapidly because a familiar pattern already supports an expectation.**", "- **Automatic use of past experience is still based on a relationship between earlier observations and a present inference.**"]}, {"id": 315, "a": ["**Heuristics** are **shortcuts that help people reach conclusions rapidly** when they use **past experience to guide present behavior.**", "- They are **rules of thumb that are likely to provide a correct answer but are not foolproof.**", "- **The shortcut reduces the amount of information a person has to consider before responding.** The cue often fits well enough to support a useful conclusion, but it can also point away from the correct judgment."]}, {"id": 316, "a": ["**The availability heuristic uses how readily examples come to mind**, while **the representativeness heuristic uses how much a case resembles a category.**", "- **Both allow rapid judgment from a limited basis.**", "- **Their difference concerns which property supplies the shortcut**: **remembered accessibility or similarity to a familiar kind of case.**"]}, {"id": 317, "a": ["The **availability heuristic** makes **events that are more easily remembered seem more probable than events that are harder to remember.**", "- **The more examples a person can think of, the more probable the event is judged to be.**", "- This can be useful when readily remembered examples reflect frequent events. **The difficulty is that the ability to remember an event is affected by properties other than its actual probability.**"]}, {"id": 318, "a": ["**Distinctiveness** makes an event **stand out from others.** **Emotional salience** makes an event **especially noticeable because of its emotional impact.**", "- **Frequent encoding or retrieval** means that an event has **repeatedly been stored or brought back to mind.**", "- **Each can increase availability without making the underlying event more common.**"]}, {"id": 319, "a": ["The word judgment asks **whether English contains more words that begin with r or more words with r as the third letter.**", "- **Both alternatives concern how often a letter occurs in a particular position.**", "- **Answering accurately requires comparing the sizes of those two sets of words.**"]}, {"id": 320, "a": ["**About 70% of respondents choose words beginning with r**, even though **words with r in the third position are actually three times more common.**", "- **The more popular response therefore reverses the underlying frequency relationship.**"]}, {"id": 321, "a": ["**Retrieving words by their first letter is easier than searching for a letter in the third position.**", "- **Words beginning with r come to mind readily, so that set feels more numerous.**", "- The availability heuristic connects that retrieval difference to the error: respondents **judge frequency through examples they can recall**, and the search cue favors one set. The mistake concerns **how memory is searched**, and not an actual excess of words beginning with the letter."]}, {"id": 322, "a": ["The paired judgments ask **which of two causes would be more likely to kill a randomly selected person in the United States during the following year.**", "- The alternatives include **homicide versus appendicitis, drowning versus an auto–train collision, asthma versus botulism, asthma versus a tornado, and appendicitis versus pregnancy.**", "- **Each choice concerns relative likelihood rather than how frightening a cause sounds.**"]}, {"id": 323, "a": ["**A death from botulism can become front-page news, while deaths from asthma can go virtually unnoticed.**", "- **The difference in exposure supplies more accessible examples of the unusual cause**, so a person remembering the news can judge that cause as more probable than its actual frequency warrants.", "- **The judgment error arises when memory accessibility is treated as a direct measure of the chance of dying from the cause.**"]}, {"id": 324, "a": ["**Asthma is more likely than botulism or a tornado**, yet **many respondents select the less likely cause.**", "- **A dramatic event can be highly available in memory even when the more common cause attracts little attention.**"]}, {"id": 325, "a": ["The **representativeness heuristic** **bases a judgment on how much one event resembles another.** A person **estimates whether A belongs to class B from how well A’s properties resemble the properties normally associated with B.**", "- **Similarity supplies the basis for category membership.**"]}, {"id": 326, "a": ["**Robert wears glasses, speaks quietly, and reads a lot.** After hearing that description, **many people judge him more likely to be a librarian than a farmer.**", "- **The description resembles their expectations about a librarian**, so it makes that occupation seem like the better match.", "- **Robert can resemble a librarian without the descriptive match settling how likely the occupation is relative to the alternative.**"]}, {"id": 327, "a": ["The **base rate** is **the background prevalence of the categories.** The prevalence information given is **about 2.2 million farms and 120,000 libraries in America.**", "- **Base-rate neglect occurs when the similarity-based response displaces the category-frequency information.**", "- **The error concerns treating a fitting description as sufficient for a probability judgment.**"]}, {"id": 328, "a": ["**A group contains 70 lawyers and 30 engineers.** **If a person is selected at random and no other information is supplied, the chance of selecting an engineer is 30 out of 100.**", "- **Respondents generally use the base-rate information correctly when it is all they have.**"]}, {"id": 329, "a": ["**Jack is described as a 45-year-old married man with four children who is conservative, careful, and ambitious**, **uninterested in political and social issues**, and who **enjoys home carpentry, sailing, and mathematical puzzles.**", "- **Respondents tend to call Jack an engineer** because those characteristics resemble their expectations about that occupation.", "- **The pattern is to use base rates when they are all that is available, then disregard them when descriptive information is available**, even though the group still contains more lawyers."]}, {"id": 330, "a": ["The **conjunction rule** states that **the probability of two events occurring together cannot be greater than the probability of either constituent event alone.**", "- **Every case satisfying both conditions also satisfies each individual condition.**", "- **Adding a requirement restricts the set of possible cases rather than enlarging it.**"]}, {"id": 331, "a": ["**Linda is described as single, outspoken, bright, trained in philosophy, concerned with discrimination and social justice, and involved in antinuclear demonstrations.** **Respondents choose between Linda is a bank teller and Linda is a bank teller and active in the feminist movement.**", "- **About 82% choose the conjunction**, because the added description **makes that answer resemble the person they have been told about.**", "- **The set of feminist bank tellers lies inside the larger set of bank tellers**, so **Linda belonging to the smaller set automatically puts her in the larger one.** The error is **choosing the more detailed, better-fitting story over the probability relationship between the two nested sets.**"]}, {"id": 332, "a": ["**Two hospitals record the proportion of boys born each day.** **The larger hospital has about 45 births per day, and the smaller hospital has about 15.** **About half of all babies are boys**, but the daily percentage can vary above or below that proportion.", "- The question asks **which hospital records more days when over 60% of the babies are boys.** The alternatives are **the larger hospital, the smaller hospital, or about the same number of days.**"]}, {"id": 333, "a": ["**Most respondents, 56%, choose about the same.**", "- That answer **treats the shared overall proportion of boys as enough to determine how often each hospital exceeds the daily threshold.**", "- **It leaves out the difference between observing a proportion in 15 births and observing it in 45 births.** **Sample size affects how much an observed proportion varies.**"]}, {"id": 334, "a": ["**The smaller hospital is the correct answer.** **Its daily sample contains about 15 births rather than 45.** **Smaller samples vary more from one observation to another**, so an extreme proportion is more likely in the smaller daily group.", "- **Both hospitals draw from a population in which about 50% of babies are boys**, but **that common population proportion does not make their day-to-day variation identical.**", "- **The larger hospital’s daily proportion is more stable because it includes more births.**"]}, {"id": 335, "a": ["**Choosing the larger hospital because it has more babies confuses an absolute count with the frequency of an extreme proportion.**", "- **The question asks which hospital more often crosses the 60% threshold, not which produces more boys in total.**"]}, {"id": 336, "a": ["The **law of large numbers** states that **as more individuals are randomly drawn from a population, the resulting group becomes more representative of that population.**", "- **A larger random sample is more likely to reflect the population pattern than a small random sample.**", "- **Increasing the number reduces the influence of short runs on the aggregate result.**"]}, {"id": 337, "a": ["**People often assume that representativeness holds for small samples as well.** **They expect a small group to resemble the population closely simply because it was drawn from that population.**", "- **That expectation understates the amount of variation possible when few observations are involved.**", "- **Treating every small sample as a miniature copy of the population leads to errors** in judgments about its likely composition."]}, {"id": 338, "a": ["An **illusory correlation** is **a relationship that appears to exist even though it does not exist or is much weaker than assumed.**", "- **The person perceives two things as strongly related without evidence supporting that degree of association.**", "- **An expectation about the relationship can shape which observations receive attention.**"]}, {"id": 339, "a": ["**Stereotypes** are **oversimplified generalizations about a group or class of people, often emphasizing negative characteristics.**", "- **A stereotype can direct attention toward behaviors that fit the expected description.** Those behaviors become **especially noticeable** while other behavior contributes less to the person’s impression.", "- **Selective attention can create an apparent association between the group and the stereotyped behavior**, and **remembering matching instances reinforces the original expectation.**"]}, {"id": 340, "a": ["**Stereotype-consistent behaviors are easier to bring to mind because they received particular attention.**", "- **Their availability can then be mistaken for evidence that the behavior is strongly associated with the group.**", "- The **accessibility of those selected examples** is what links the illusory correlation to the availability heuristic."]}, {"id": 341, "a": ["**Lord and colleagues (1979)** had **people who favored capital punishment and people who opposed it read the same article.**", "- **The participants began with different opinions, but the material they evaluated was shared.**", "- **Their judgments therefore concerned the same information approached from opposing positions.**"]}, {"id": 342, "a": ["In Lord and colleagues (1979), **those in favor of capital punishment interpreted the article as favorable to their position**, and **those against capital punishment interpreted it as favorable to theirs.**", "- **The existing attitude influenced how the evidence was evaluated**, rather than the shared article producing one common interpretation.", "- **Participants focused on information that agreed with their beliefs and disregarded information that did not.**"]}, {"id": 343, "a": ["The **myside bias** is **the tendency to generate and evaluate evidence, and to test hypotheses, in ways biased toward one’s own opinions and attitudes.**", "- **The person gives greater weight to information that agrees with the position already held.**"]}, {"id": 344, "a": ["**Confirmation bias** is **the tendency to seek information that fits a hypothesis and overlook information that argues against it.**", "- **The myside bias is one form of this pattern.**", "- **Evidence is selected or evaluated in a way that favors the account already believed.**"]}, {"id": 345, "a": ["In the **number-rule task**, participants receive **2, 4, 6 as a sequence that conforms to a hidden rule.**", "- **They write their own sets of three numbers and give reasons for choosing them.**", "- **For each set, they receive feedback only about whether it fits the experimenter’s rule, then propose the rule when confident.**"]}, {"id": 346, "a": ["**The most common hypothesis is increasing intervals of two.** **Testing only sequences that satisfy that hypothesis can produce repeated positive feedback.**", "- **The actual rule is three numbers in increasing order of magnitude.** **Equal intervals of two are unnecessary because any ascending triple satisfies it.**", "- **Those successful tests also fit a broader rule, so confirmation alone does not distinguish the narrow hypothesis from another account.** **Searching for possible contradictions would test the boundaries of the hypothesis.**"]}, {"id": 347, "a": ["**Utility** refers to **outcomes that are desirable because they serve the person’s interests.** **It expresses how beneficial an outcome is to the decision maker.**", "- **Desirability gives the outcomes value, while uncertainty concerns how likely those outcomes are to occur.**"]}, {"id": 348, "a": ["**Expected utility theory assumes that people are rational and, with all relevant information, choose the alternative with maximum expected utility.**", "- **The judgment combines the value of possible outcomes with their chances** rather than choosing solely by how attractive one outcome sounds.", "- **The monetary version emphasizes the maximum expected monetary payoff.** **A large possible payoff and a likely payoff are different features of a choice, and expected value depends on their combination.**"]}, {"id": 349, "a": ["**People can find value in things other than money.** **Maximizing monetary payoff therefore does not capture every consideration that can make an outcome desirable.**", "- **Many decisions also fail to maximize the probability of the best outcome.** **People are not utility-maximization machines whose behavior always follows the calculated best monetary prospect.**", "- **The attractive feature of a choice can influence behavior even when the numerical odds favor another response.**"]}, {"id": 350, "a": ["**Casino gambling is an example in which people continue to choose an activity despite knowing that the odds are strongly against them.**", "- **Knowing the unfavorable odds does not automatically eliminate the choice.**", "- **Actual decision behavior therefore involves considerations that extend beyond selecting the maximum expected monetary payoff.**"]}, {"id": 351, "a": ["**The utility approach supplies specific procedures for determining the best choice according to expected benefit.**", "- **It offers a consistent basis for comparing alternatives when their outcomes and probabilities are available.**", "- **The chosen alternative is the one that maximizes the relevant expected value.**"]}, {"id": 352, "a": ["**Participants can earn one dollar whenever they draw a red jellybean**, with an opportunity to earn up to seven dollars. **They choose between two bowls before drawing.**", "- **One bowl offers 1 red bean out of 10, while the other offers 7 red beans out of 100.** **The first chance is 10%, and the second is 7%.**", "- **The relevant outcome is drawing a red bean, so the proportion of red beans determines the chance of winning on a draw.**"]}, {"id": 353, "a": ["**Most participants choose the 7-out-of-100 bowl.** **The greater number of red beans is attractive even though the probability of drawing one is worse.**", "- **Comparing the winning counts alone favors seven, while comparing the complete ratios favors one out of ten.**", "- **A larger number of favorable items can draw attention away from the denominator, the total number of possible draws**, so the decision depends on considerations other than the actual probabilities of winning."]}, {"id": 354, "a": ["**After September 11, air travel decreased and driving increased.** **Some people avoided flying by choosing road travel instead.**", "- **The decision changed the mode of transportation rather than removing the risks associated with traveling.**", "- **The probability of dying in a car accident is much greater than the probability of dying while flying.**"]}, {"id": 355, "a": ["**Avoiding the feared event can increase exposure to a more likely danger.** **The reported number of Americans who died on the road while avoiding flying exceeded the total number of passengers killed on the four hijacked flights.**", "- **The emotional impact of the air attacks and the relative probabilities of the travel alternatives are different bases for a decision.**", "- **The risk comparison requires considering both alternatives.**"]}, {"id": 356, "a": ["In the **convoy dilemma**, **a convoy of food trucks is traveling to a refugee camp during a famine, and airplanes cannot be used.** **A second camp contains more refugees.**", "- **Rerouting the convoy saves 1,000 people at the second camp, but 100 people in the first camp die because the trucks no longer arrive there.**", "- **Counting lives saved against lives lost gives 100 − 1,000 = −900 for retaining the original route and 1,000 − 100 = 900 for rerouting.** Under that comparison, rerouting has the better net outcome."]}, {"id": 357, "a": ["**Ritov and Baron (1999)** found that **63% did not choose to reroute.**", "- **Most respondents rejected the alternative with the better net count of lives saved**, so their decision **could not be described solely by choosing the larger numerical benefit.**", "- **Some respondents would retain the original route even if only one person at the first camp would die after the change.** **Reluctance to cause that loss can outweigh the larger number saved elsewhere.**"]}, {"id": 358, "a": ["The **Sacred Value Protection Model of Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green, and Lerner (2000)** proposes that **people want to protect what they hold sacred.**", "- **A sacred value is treated as something whose protection cannot be reduced to an ordinary exchange for material advantage.**", "- **Sacred values are compartmentalized separately from secular values.** **Secular values can enter ordinary practical exchanges**, while sacred values belong to a different domain."]}, {"id": 359, "a": ["A **taboo tradeoff** **intermingles the sacred and secular domains.** **Treating a sacred concern as exchangeable for a secular benefit can feel threatening and provoke moral outrage.**", "- A **tragic tradeoff** **compares two sacred values with one another.** **People are less morally outraged by that kind of conflict because either choice protects one sacred concern at the expense of another.**", "- **The objection to a taboo tradeoff concerns the kind of comparison being made**, rather than only whether the offered benefit is large enough."]}, {"id": 360, "a": ["In the **taboo tradeoff**, **Robert can save Johnny, a five-year-old child who needs a liver transplant.** **The procedure costs the hospital one million dollars.** The money could instead support **better equipment and salaries to recruit talented doctors.**", "- In the **tragic tradeoff**, **two children need a liver transplant, but only one liver is available.**", "- **Only the taboo version places the sacred value of a child’s life against a monetary allocation.** The tragic version makes **the scarcity of an organ create a conflict between two equally life-preserving aims.**"]}, {"id": 361, "a": ["In **Tetlock and colleagues’ (2000)** results, **under the taboo tradeoff, not saving Johnny produces much greater outrage than saving him.** **Choosing other uses for the money instead of the child’s life receives the strongest negative response.**", "- **Under the tragic tradeoff, outrage is similar whether Johnny is saved or not**, because **choosing either child leaves another sacred concern unmet.**", "- **The findings concern an interaction between the type of value conflict and the decision made within it.**"]}, {"id": 362, "a": ["**Ginges, Atran, Medin, and Shikaki (2007)** examined **sacred-value tradeoffs in a setting involving an ongoing political conflict.**", "- **They interviewed Israeli settlers, Palestinian refugees, and Palestinian students**, with samples of **601 settlers, 535 refugees, and 719 students.**", "- **Participants were offered three types of tradeoff deals as possible solutions.**"]}, {"id": 363, "a": ["In **Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)**, the **taboo proposal** asks **Palestinians to recognize the sacred and historic right of the Jewish people to Israel**, with **two states, a Jewish state of Israel and a Palestinian state in 99% of the West Bank and Gaza.**", "- The **Taboo+ version adds a material incentive**: **Israel would pay Palestine one billion U.S. dollars a year for ten years.**", "- The **symbolic version adds recognition by Israel of the historic and legitimate right of Palestinians to their own state**, and **an apology for wrongs done to the Palestinian people.**"]}, {"id": 364, "a": ["**Moral absolutists treat an issue under dispute as a sacred value.** **Their position is that the proposed compromise is unacceptable regardless of how much benefit it could produce.**", "- **Non-absolutists also have strong preferences against compromise but do not treat the issues as sacred.** **They are not simply indifferent to the dispute.**", "- **The distinction concerns whether consequences and benefits can enter the decision at all**, rather than whether the person cares about the issue."]}, {"id": 365, "a": ["In **Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)**, **among the settlers, about 46% held that they should never give up part of the Land of Israel, no matter how great the benefit.**", "- **Among the Palestinian students, about 54% treated both the right of return and Jerusalem as sacred values.**", "- **The issue regarded as sacred can differ between groups**, while **the refusal to exchange it for benefits supplies a common basis for the classification.**"]}, {"id": 366, "a": ["In **Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)**, for **moral absolutists**, **adding money in Taboo+ increases anger and disgust relative to the basic taboo offer**, and **support for violence also increases.**", "- **Non-absolutists respond in the opposite direction**: **adding the material benefit reduces anger and disgust and lowers support for violence.**", "- **The same change to the offer therefore has different effects depending on whether participants treat the contested issue as a sacred value.**"]}, {"id": 367, "a": ["In **Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)**, **the comparison of taboo and symbolic offers associates symbolic recognition with lower anger or disgust and lower support for violence.**", "- **Acknowledgment of a group’s claim affects responses differently from offering money for compromise.**", "- **The symbolic term addresses a value that the material exchange can leave threatened.**"]}, {"id": 368, "a": ["In **Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)**, the findings **distinguish material benefit from symbolic reciprocity.** The **Taboo+** version added **money**, and the **symbolic** version added **recognition and an apology.**", "- **Increasing an economic incentive is not automatically an improvement in a negotiation over sacred concerns**: for absolutists, money raised anger, disgust and support for violence.", "- **What the offer asks people to exchange, and how it treats their commitments, affects the emotional and behavioral judgments they report.**"]}, {"id": 369, "a": ["**Material incentives and disincentives are possible tools in negotiations involving sacred values.**", "- **An incentive offers a benefit for agreement**, while **a disincentive imposes a cost for refusing.**", "- **Economic funds change the benefits of accepting, while economic sanctions, a disincentive, change the costs of refusing.**"]}, {"id": 370, "a": ["**Dehghani, Atran, and colleagues (2010)** and **Dehghani, Iliev, and colleagues (2009)** investigated **Iran’s nuclear program.**", "- **The question was whether people who treated the program as a sacred value would be amenable to material benefits or costs in exchange for compromise.**", "- **A commitment that excludes ordinary benefit-based tradeoffs can be evaluated differently from a negotiable preference.**"]}, {"id": 371, "a": ["In the study by Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), **the sacred-value measure, based on Baron and Spranca (1997), asks what participants think about Iran giving up its nuclear energy or weapons program.**", "- **Responses range from saying it definitely should happen, through not objecting, to accepting it only if the benefits are great enough.**", "- **The sacred-value response is that giving up the program should not be done no matter how great the consequences.** **Participants choosing that alternative are classified as having a sacred value.**"]}, {"id": 372, "a": ["In **Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)**, **the online study involved 1,997 Iranian participants and was conducted in Farsi.**", "- **The measured outcomes included approval, anger, and whether a proposal was considered misleading.**", "- **About 14% for nuclear energy and 13% for nuclear weapons select the sacred-value response.** **The classification identifies a subgroup within the participants.**"]}, {"id": 373, "a": ["In **Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)**, **the experiment uses a 2 × 3 design.** **The nuclear program concerns either energy or weapons, and the deal is Taboo, Taboo+, or Taboo−.**", "- **Combining two program types with three deal types yields six conditions.**", "- **Participants rate whether they personally approve, whether people would be angry if the Iranian government approved, and whether the proposed deal seems misleading.**"]}, {"id": 374, "a": ["In **Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)**, the **Taboo agreement** has **Iran give up its relevant nuclear program and surrender its current facilities to the United Nations**, and **in return, Israel gives up the corresponding nuclear program and surrenders its facilities.**", "- **Taboo+ adds 40 billion dollars in economic funds from the United Nations to Iran.**", "- **Taboo− adds the threat of additional sanctions if Iran refuses the deal.** **The additions respectively change the material benefit of agreement and the cost of refusal.**"]}, {"id": 375, "a": ["In **Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)**, for participants treating **nuclear energy as a sacred value**, **approval is lower with the financial incentive in Taboo+ than with the basic taboo deal**, and **the sanctions condition, Taboo−, also has lower approval than the baseline.**", "- **Among sacred-value participants, anticipated anger is highest with the financial incentive and is also elevated with the sanctions threat relative to the baseline.**", "- **The highest approval and lowest anger within this group belong to the unmodified offer.** **A financial addition can backfire rather than purchase agreement.**"]}, {"id": 376, "a": ["In **Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)**, **participants without a sacred value have much higher approval and lower anger across the energy conditions.**", "- **Their ratings vary relatively little between the baseline, financial incentive, and sanctions versions.**", "- **The large separation in responses concerns whether nuclear energy is treated as sacred.**"]}, {"id": 377, "a": ["**Induction draws a probable conclusion from observed evidence**, while **heuristics are shortcuts that use past experience and cues**, such as availability or similarity.", "- **Heuristics are likely to give a correct answer but are not foolproof.**", "- Both use past experience, but induction is described as reasoning from evidence and heuristics as quick rules of thumb."]}, {"id": 378, "a": ["**Availability substitutes ease of remembering for probability**, as in the **r-words and causes of death.**", "- **Representativeness substitutes resemblance for probability**, ignoring **base rates** in **Robert and Jack**, and the **conjunction rule** in **Linda.**", "- Each heuristic is quick and often useful, and each produces errors when the cue it relies on is not the same as the real probability."]}, {"id": 379, "a": ["In both **Robert and Jack**, **a description that resembles a category displaces the background frequencies of the categories.**", "- That is **base-rate neglect**: **the similarity-based response displaces the category-frequency information.**"]}, {"id": 380, "a": ["Each response follows **how well a description fits a category**, and not the probability relationship.", "- In **Linda**, the relationship that is ignored is **the nested sets**: feminist bank tellers lie inside bank tellers.", "- In **Jack**, it is **the group composition** of 70 lawyers and 30 engineers."]}, {"id": 381, "a": ["The **conjunction rule** concerns **nested sets**, since **adding a condition cannot raise probability.**", "- The **law of large numbers** concerns **sample size and representativeness**: **larger random samples become more representative of the population.**"]}, {"id": 382, "a": ["**Answering about the same ignores the number of births behind each percentage.** It treats the shared overall proportion of boys as enough, and leaves out the difference between observing a proportion in **15 births** and in **45 births.**", "- **People often assume that representativeness holds for small samples as well**, expecting a small group to resemble the population closely.", "- But **small samples vary more and produce extreme proportions more often**, which is why **the smaller hospital** has more days with over 60% boys. This is the **law of large numbers**: larger random samples are more representative."]}, {"id": 383, "a": ["**Stereotype-consistent behaviors get attention and are easier to bring to mind.**", "- **That availability is mistaken for strong evidence of association.**", "- The illusory correlation therefore depends on **which examples are accessible.**"]}, {"id": 384, "a": ["**Confirmation bias** is **the broad tendency to seek information that fits a hypothesis and overlook information that argues against it.**", "- **Myside bias** is **one form of it**: **generating and evaluating evidence, and testing hypotheses, in ways biased toward one’s own opinions and attitudes.**", "- Confirmation bias covers any hypothesis, and myside bias is the case where the hypothesis is what the person already believes, as in **Lord and colleagues (1979)**, where each group read the same article as favorable to its own position."]}, {"id": 385, "a": ["In **Lord and colleagues (1979)**, **the same article was read as favorable to each side.**", "- In the **2, 4, 6 task**, **testing only sequences that fit the hypothesis gives repeated positive feedback and leaves the narrow hypothesis intact.**", "- Both show **evidence being used in a way that favors the account already believed.**"]}, {"id": 386, "a": ["**Trying sequences that violate the hypothesis tests its boundaries.**", "- **Confirming triples leave the narrower belief intact.**", "- **Searching for possible contradictions** is what would show that the actual rule is any three numbers in increasing order."]}, {"id": 387, "a": ["**Expected utility theory says people choose the maximum expected utility**, combining the value of outcomes with their chances.", "- **But people value things beyond money and gamble against the odds.**", "- **Actual behavior therefore departs from the theory**, which is why people are not described as utility-maximization machines."]}, {"id": 388, "a": ["In the **jellybean problem**, **the larger number of red beans drew attention away from the denominator.**", "- In the **hospital problem**, **the answer about the same left out how many births were behind each day’s percentage.**", "- In both, **part of the information is overlooked**, and the judgment follows what stands out."]}, {"id": 389, "a": ["In the **causes-of-death comparisons**, **distinctive coverage made an unusual cause more available than a more likely one**: **botulism could be front-page news while asthma deaths go virtually unnoticed**, so people judged botulism more probable.", "- **After September 11, perceived danger from the attacks can guide travel choices toward the more probable danger of the road**, and **the number who died on the road exceeded the passengers killed on the four hijacked flights.**", "- In both, **decisions departed from actual probability**, because something other than probability, news coverage or emotional impact, drove the judgment."]}, {"id": 390, "a": ["In **Ritov and Baron (1999)**, **counting lives saved says reroute, but 63 percent did not**, and **reluctance to cause a loss can outweigh the larger number saved.**", "- **Expected utility** combines value and chance and **chooses the maximum.**", "- The two ways of evaluating outcomes therefore give different answers."]}, {"id": 391, "a": ["A **taboo tradeoff compares a sacred value with a secular benefit**, while a **tragic tradeoff compares two sacred values.**", "- **People are less morally outraged by the tragic kind**, because either choice protects one sacred concern."]}, {"id": 392, "a": ["In **Tetlock and colleagues (2000)**, **outrage depended on the outcome only in the taboo condition**, where **not saving Johnny was far worse.**", "- **In the tragic condition, outrage was similar for both outcomes.**", "- That is the interaction between **the type of value conflict and the decision made.**"]}, {"id": 393, "a": ["In the **Johnny scenarios** of **Tetlock and colleagues (2000)**, **outrage depended on the type of tradeoff**: much greater for not saving Johnny in the taboo case, similar for both outcomes in the tragic case.", "- In the **convoy dilemma** of **Ritov and Baron (1999)**, **63% did not choose to reroute** even though rerouting had the better net count, and **reluctance to cause a loss can outweigh the larger number saved.**", "- In both, **the responses were not a simple numerical comparison** of lives saved against lives lost."]}, {"id": 394, "a": ["In **Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)**, **moral absolutists treat the issue as a sacred value**, so **the basic deal is already a taboo proposal.** **Adding money in Taboo+ makes an exchange they already treat as sacred more objectionable**, and it **increases anger, disgust and support for violence.**", "- **Non-absolutists respond in the opposite direction**: **adding the material benefit reduces anger and disgust and lowers support for violence.**", "- The same change to the offer has different effects depending on **whether participants treat the contested issue as a sacred value.**"]}, {"id": 395, "a": ["In **Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)**, **symbolic recognition addresses the status of the group’s sacred claim**, so it was associated with **lower anger and lower support for violence.**", "- **Money engages a material exchange**, which is a different kind of offer."]}, {"id": 396, "a": ["**Both compare a taboo deal with versions that add material benefit or cost.**", "- Ginges and colleagues added **money** in Taboo+ and **symbolic recognition**, while Dehghani and colleagues added **funds** in Taboo+ and **sanctions** in Taboo−.", "- **Both separate participants who hold the issue sacred from those who do not.**"]}, {"id": 397, "a": ["In **Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)**, **an incentive offers a benefit for agreement** and **a disincentive imposes a cost for refusing**: **economic funds change the benefits of accepting, and economic sanctions change the costs of refusing.** Both try to alter the practical consequences of the decision.", "- For participants with a sacred value about **nuclear energy**, **approval was lower with the financial incentive and with sanctions than with the baseline**, and **anticipated anger was highest with the incentive and elevated with sanctions.**", "- Changing the material consequences did not make the compromise more attractive to those who held the program sacred."]}, {"id": 398, "a": ["**Expected utility theory combines the value of outcomes with their chances**, so a larger benefit is predicted to raise the expected utility.", "- **For moral absolutists in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), adding money increased anger, disgust and support for violence.**", "- For absolutists, the compromise is **unacceptable regardless of how much benefit it could produce.**"]}, {"id": 399, "a": ["**Emotions take part in decision making** rather than only getting in its way. **People with damage to an area of their prefrontal cortex, who suffer from flattened emotions, have impaired decision making.**", "- **Flattened emotions are emotions that are weakened, so the person feels much less than usual.**", "- **When emotion is reduced in this way, decisions get worse rather than better.**"]}, {"id": 400, "a": ["**Anxious people tend to avoid making decisions that could potentially lead to large negative consequences.** This pattern is **risk avoidance.**", "- **A choice that carries the chance of a large loss feels too threatening to an anxious person**, so that option is passed up **even when it might turn out well.**"]}, {"id": 401, "a": ["**Optimistic people are more likely to ignore negative information and focus on positive information.**", "- **Some optimism can be useful, but too much optimism can lead to poor decision making.**", "- **A person who sets aside the negative information about a choice is deciding without the whole picture**, so the decision is more likely to go wrong."]}, {"id": 402, "a": ["**Expected emotions** are **emotions that people predict they will feel concerning an outcome.**", "- **Before choosing, a person imagines how a good result or a bad result would feel.**", "- **Those predicted feelings then guide which option the person is willing to take.**"]}, {"id": 403, "a": ["**Expected emotions are one of the determinants of risk aversion**, and **risk aversion is the tendency to avoid taking risks.**", "- **A person who expects to feel very bad after a loss is less willing to accept a chance of that loss.**", "- **The prediction about feelings, rather than the outcome alone, pushes the person toward the safer option.**"]}, {"id": 404, "a": ["**People inaccurately predict their emotions.** In particular, **people show a tendency to believe that a particular loss will have a greater impact than a gain of the same size**, and **that belief increases risk aversion.**", "- **Because a loss is expected to hurt more than an equal gain would help, people turn down risks that would be reasonable if the two were weighed evenly.**"]}, {"id": 405, "a": ["**Kermer and colleagues (2006)** **compared people’s expected emotions with their actual emotions.**", "- **Participants were given $5 and told that, based on a coin flip, they would either win an additional $5 or lose $3.**", "- **They rated their happiness before the experiment started, and that rating served as each person’s baseline.**"]}, {"id": 406, "a": ["In Kermer and colleagues (2006), **before the coin flip, participants expected losing $3 to lower their happiness by about 4 points below baseline and winning $5 to raise it by about 2 points.**", "- **The expected loss was about twice the size of the expected gain, even though the loss was the smaller amount of money.**"]}, {"id": 407, "a": ["In **Kermer and colleagues (2006)**, **after the coin flip, the actual drop from losing was only about 1 point, and the actual rise from winning was about 1.2 points.**", "- **Participants overestimated their negative feeling, since losing hurt far less than they had predicted.**", "- **People usually have coping mechanisms that get activated, but they do not consider them when predicting emotions.** **Those coping mechanisms soften the real feeling after a loss, which is why the prediction misses.**"]}, {"id": 408, "a": ["**Incidental emotions** are **emotions that are not specifically related to decision making.** **They may be related to one’s general disposition or personality, some recent experience, or one’s general environment or surroundings.**", "- **A disposition is a person’s usual emotional tendency**, **a recent experience can leave a mood behind**, and **the surroundings, such as the weather, can change how a person feels while deciding.**"]}, {"id": 409, "a": ["**Simonsohn (2007)** analyzed **university admissions decisions** and found that **applicants’ academic attributes were more heavily weighted on cloudy days than on sunny days.**", "- **The weather had nothing to do with the applicants.**", "- **It still changed what the people making the decisions paid the most attention to.**"]}, {"id": 410, "a": ["**Simonsohn (2009)** found that **prospective students visiting an academically highly rated university were more likely to enroll if they had visited the campus on a cloudy day.**", "- **The weather on the day of the visit is unrelated to the quality of the school.**", "- **These findings place weather among the incidental factors that can carry into important decisions.**"]}, {"id": 411, "a": ["**Lerner and colleagues (2004)** had participants **view one of three film clips.**", "- **The sad condition clip was of a person dying, the disgust condition clip was of a person using a dirty toilet, and the neutral clip was of fish at the Great Barrier Reef.**", "- **Participants were then given a set of highlighters and asked to determine a price**: **what they would be willing to sell the highlighters for, and what they would be willing to pay to buy them.**"]}, {"id": 412, "a": ["For the **selling price**, in Lerner and colleagues (2004), **the neutral group asked about $4.50, the disgust group about $2.70 and the sad group about $3.00.**", "- **The clips had nothing to do with highlighters**, so any difference in price came from **the mood the clip produced.**"]}, {"id": 413, "a": ["For the **buying price**, in Lerner and colleagues (2004), **the neutral group offered about $3.50, the disgust group about $3.00 and the sad group about $4.50.**", "- **Disgust lowered both prices, while sadness lowered the selling price and raised the buying price above the neutral group.**", "- **The emotions came from film clips that were unrelated to the choice, which makes them incidental emotions.** **Even so, they changed how much the same object seemed worth.**"]}, {"id": 414, "a": ["**Decisions can depend on the context within which they are made.** **Physicians were presented with a hypothetical test case involving a possible candidate for cesarean section.** **The test case itself was always the same, and only what came before it changed across three contexts.**", "- **In the control context, the test case was presented first.** **In the serious previous cases context, it was preceded by four other cases in which there were serious complications.** **In the not serious previous cases context, it was preceded by four other cases that were fairly routine.**", "- **Significantly more doctors recommended cesarean after reading about non-serious cases first.** **The cases read just before it changed how the same patient was judged.**"]}, {"id": 415, "a": ["**Decisions depend on how choices are presented.** **In an opt-in procedure, a person must take an active step to be an organ donor**, and **countries with opt-in procedures have less than a 28% consent rate.**", "- **In an opt-out procedure, a person is an organ donor unless they request not to be**, and **countries with opt-out procedures have over a 99% consent rate.**", "- **Since many people do nothing either way, the consent rates end up far apart.**"]}, {"id": 416, "a": ["The **status quo bias** is **the tendency to do nothing when faced with a difficult decision.**", "- **Under opt-in, doing nothing leaves a person as a non-donor, and under opt-out, doing nothing leaves a person as a donor.**", "- **Since many people do nothing either way, the consent rates end up far apart.**"]}, {"id": 417, "a": ["In the short story **The International Community**, **a chicken, a duck, a turkey, a pheasant, a quail and a partridge were summoned and traveled together to the summit**, where **the king’s chef told them he had called them so they could tell him what sauce they would like to be eaten with.**", "- **When one bird said it did not want to be eaten at all, the chef answered that that is out of the question**, so **the only choice offered was which sauce.**", "- It presents **a choice of the same kind** as the presentation of options in the donation example."]}, {"id": 418, "a": ["The **framing effect** is **when decisions are influenced by how a decision is stated.**", "- **The way a choice is stated can highlight one aspect of the situation, such as what might be gained or what might be lost.**", "- **The facts of the choice can stay the same while the statement changes what people focus on.**"]}, {"id": 419, "a": ["**Tversky and Kahneman (1981)** studied **how framing changes the amount of risk people accept.**", "- **When situations are framed in terms of gains, people tend toward a risk-aversion strategy**, which means they **prefer the sure option.**", "- **When situations are framed in terms of losses, people tend toward a risk-taking strategy**, which means they **prefer the gamble.**"]}, {"id": 420, "a": ["A **risk-aversion strategy** **avoids an uncertain outcome in favor of a certain one.**", "- A **risk-taking strategy** **accepts an uncertain outcome in the hope of avoiding a certain loss.**", "- **The same people can switch between the two strategies depending only on whether the outcomes are described as gains or as losses.**"]}, {"id": 421, "a": ["In the problem in **Tversky and Kahneman (1981)**, **a disease is expected to kill 600 people**, and **two alternative programs to combat the disease have been proposed.** **The outcomes are stated in terms of people saved, which makes this the gain frame.**", "- **With Program A, 200 people will be saved for certain.** **With Program B, there is a 1/3 probability that 600 people will be saved and a 2/3 probability that no people will be saved.**", "- **Program A is the sure option, and Program B is the gamble.**"]}, {"id": 422, "a": ["In **Tversky and Kahneman (1981)**, **72% chose Program A, and 28% chose Program B.**", "- **When the outcomes were described as lives saved, most people avoided the risk and took the certain result.** **That choice fits the risk-aversion strategy that the gain frame encourages.**", "- **Choosing A means giving up the chance to save everyone in exchange for certainty.**"]}, {"id": 423, "a": ["In **Tversky and Kahneman (1981)**, In the **loss frame**, **the same disease is expected to kill 600 people**, and **the outcomes are stated in terms of people who will die.**", "- **With Program C, 400 people will die for certain.** **With Program D, there is a 1/3 probability that no one will die and a 2/3 probability that 600 people will die.**", "- **Program C is the sure option, and Program D is the gamble.**"]}, {"id": 424, "a": ["In **Tversky and Kahneman (1981)**, **78% chose Program D, and 22% chose Program C.**", "- **Program C has the same outcome as Program A**, since **saving 200 of 600 people means that 400 die**, and **Program D is the same gamble as Program B.**", "- **Only the wording changed, yet the majority switched from the sure option to the gamble, which is the framing effect.** **Choosing D means taking a risk to avoid a certain loss.**"]}, {"id": 425, "a": ["**Neuroeconomics** **combines research from psychology, neuroscience, and economics.**", "- **Psychology contributes how people think and decide, neuroscience contributes what happens in the brain, and economics contributes how choices about money and value are analyzed.**", "- **One finding is that decisions are influenced by emotions, and those emotions are associated with activity in specific areas of the brain.**"]}, {"id": 426, "a": ["**Sanfey and colleagues (2003)** used the **ultimatum game**, which has **two players, a proposer and a responder.** **The proposer gets $10 and makes an offer to the responder as to how this money should be split.**", "- **The responder can accept or reject the offer.** **If the responder rejects it, neither player receives anything, so a rejection costs both players.** **If the responder accepts, the money is divided the way the proposer offered.**", "- **A low offer leaves the responder deciding between a small amount of money and nothing at all.**"]}, {"id": 427, "a": ["**According to utility theory, the rational decision for the responder is to accept any offer.**", "- **Utility theory judges a choice by the value of its outcome**, and **any offer, even $1, is worth more than the nothing that comes from rejecting.**", "- **The way real responders behave can then be compared with that standard.**"]}, {"id": 428, "a": ["In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), **participants played 20 separate games, 10 with human partners and 10 with computer partners.**", "- **Offers of $5 and $5 and of $7 and $3 were accepted almost every time**, at **about 100% and about 95% for both kinds of partner.**", "- **An offer of $8 and $2 was accepted about 53% of the time from a human and about 85% from a computer.** **An offer of $9 and $1 was accepted about 37% of the time from a human and about 68% from a computer.**"]}, {"id": 429, "a": ["In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), **participants often rejected low offers because they became angry that the offers were unfair**, and **they were less angry with an unfair computer.**", "- **That is why the same low offer was accepted more often when a computer made it.**"]}, {"id": 430, "a": ["In **Sanfey and colleagues (2003)**, **participants with more activation of the right anterior insula, an area connected with emotional states, were more likely to reject more offers.**", "- **Rejecting an offer meant getting nothing**, so **the emotion linked to this area led people to give up money.**", "- **Emotion is important in decision making.**"]}, {"id": 431, "a": ["**Deductive reasoning** is **determining whether a conclusion logically follows from premises.**", "- **A premise is a statement that the reasoning starts from.**", "- **Deductive reasoning is concerned with whether the conclusion follows from those premises.**"]}, {"id": 432, "a": ["A **syllogism** is **the basic form of deductive reasoning, and it was introduced by Aristotle.** **A syllogism has two statements called premises and a third statement called the conclusion.**", "- **A categorical syllogism describes the relation between two categories using all, no, or some.**"]}, {"id": 433, "a": ["**Premise 1 is All birds are animals, Premise 2 is All animals eat food, and the conclusion is Therefore, all birds eat food.**", "- **Birds fall inside the category of animals, and animals fall inside the category of things that eat food, so birds fall inside that category too.**"]}, {"id": 434, "a": ["**A syllogism is valid if its conclusion follows logically from its two premises.**", "- **If the two premises of a valid syllogism are true, the syllogism’s conclusion must be true.**", "- **Validity depends on the form of the reasoning, while truth depends on whether the statements match the world.**"]}, {"id": 435, "a": ["**Validity should not be confused with truth.** **The syllogism All birds are animals, All animals have four legs, All birds have four legs is valid, because it has the same form as the first example.**", "- **Its conclusion is false only because the second premise is false.**", "- **A valid form with a false premise can still produce a false conclusion**, so **a valid syllogism does not guarantee a true conclusion on its own.**"]}, {"id": 436, "a": ["**Syllogisms can be invalid even though each of the premises and the conclusion seem reasonable.**", "- **In the syllogism All of the students are tired, Some tired people are irritable, Some of the students are irritable, every statement sounds reasonable.**", "- **The syllogism is still not valid, because the conclusion does not follow from the premises.**"]}, {"id": 437, "a": ["**Belief bias** is **the tendency to think that a syllogism is valid if its conclusion is believable.**", "- **71% of participants judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid.**", "- **A believable conclusion led most people to accept reasoning that did not hold**, because **good reasoning and truth are not the same thing.**"]}, {"id": 438, "a": ["**There is no easy method for determining whether a syllogism is valid or invalid.** **One way is to picture the situation the statements describe.**", "- **Most people solve the pool table problem by imagining the way the balls are arranged on the pool table.**", "- **The idea that people can imagine situations is the basis of Johnson-Laird’s proposal that people use mental models.**"]}, {"id": 439, "a": ["In the **pool table problem**, **there is a black ball directly above the cue ball, the green ball is on the right side of the cue ball, and there is a red ball between them.** **The speaker then moves so the red ball is between the speaker and the black ball.**", "- **The answer is that the cue ball is to the left of the line of sight.**", "- **Picturing the table puts the black ball above the cue ball, with the red ball and then the green ball to the cue ball’s right.** **Standing so the red ball blocks the black ball means looking past it toward the black ball**, and from there the cue ball sits off to the left."]}, {"id": 440, "a": ["In **Johnson-Laird (1999)**, **a mental model is a specific situation represented in a person’s mind that can be used to help determine the validity of syllogisms in deductive reasoning.**", "- **The person builds a picture of the situation described by the premises and reasons from that picture.**"]}, {"id": 441, "a": ["**The person first creates a model of the situation and then generates tentative conclusions about the model**, which are conclusions that might hold.", "- **Next the person looks for exceptions to falsify the model**, meaning **searching for a case where the premises are true but the conclusion is not.**", "- **Finally the person determines the validity of the syllogism from whether any exception was found.**"]}, {"id": 442, "a": ["**A conclusion is valid only if it cannot be refuted by any model of the premises.** **To refute a conclusion is to prove that it can be false.**", "- **If even one model fits the premises but not the conclusion, the conclusion does not follow.**", "- **Searching for exceptions is how a person can reject an invalid conclusion.**"]}, {"id": 443, "a": ["**Conditional syllogisms have two premises and a conclusion, and the first premise is of the form If p, then q.** **The letter p stands for a condition, and q stands for what follows when that condition is met.**", "- **All four types share the same first premise.** **They differ in the second premise, which states either p, not p, q or not q, and in the conclusion drawn from it.**", "- **Syllogism 1 goes from p to therefore q, syllogism 2 from not q to therefore not p, syllogism 3 from q to therefore p, and syllogism 4 from not p to therefore not q.**"]}, {"id": 444, "a": ["**Two of the four types are valid and two are not.**", "- **Telling them apart depends on whether the conclusion truly follows from If p, then q together with the second premise.**", "- **People judge some of the types correctly far more often than others.**"]}, {"id": 445, "a": ["**Conditional syllogism 1 is called modus ponens.** **The premises are If I study, I’ll get a good grade and I studied, and the conclusion is Therefore, I’ll get a good grade.**", "- **In letters, the first premise is If p, then q, the second premise is p, and the conclusion is therefore q.** **Since the condition p is met, what follows from it, q, has to follow too.**", "- **This syllogism is valid, and 97% of participants judged it correctly.** **Modus ponens is the easiest of the four types for people to evaluate.**"]}, {"id": 446, "a": ["**Conditional syllogism 2 is called modus tollens.** **The premises are If I study, I’ll get a good grade and I didn’t get a good grade, and the conclusion is Therefore, I didn’t study.**", "- **In letters, the second premise is not q, and the conclusion is therefore not p.** **If studying always leads to a good grade, then missing the good grade means the studying did not happen.**", "- **This syllogism is valid, but only 60% of participants judged it correctly.** **Modus tollens is just as valid as modus ponens, yet people find it much harder to evaluate.**"]}, {"id": 447, "a": ["In **conditional syllogism 3**, the premises are **If I study, I’ll get a good grade and I got a good grade**, and the conclusion is **Therefore, I studied.** **In letters, the second premise is q and the conclusion is therefore p.**", "- **This syllogism is not valid, and only 40% of participants judged it correctly.**", "- **The first premise says what happens when someone studies, but it does not say that studying is the only way to get a good grade.**"]}, {"id": 448, "a": ["**The same form with different content is If I live in Tucson, then I live in Arizona, I live in Arizona, Therefore, I live in Tucson.**", "- **With this content the conclusion is easier to see as not following**, since **a person can live in Arizona without living in Tucson.**"]}, {"id": 449, "a": ["In **conditional syllogism 4**, the premises are **If I study, I’ll get a good grade and I didn’t study**, and the conclusion is **Therefore, I didn’t get a good grade.** **In letters, the second premise is not p and the conclusion is therefore not q.**", "- **This syllogism is not valid, and only 40% of participants judged it correctly.**", "- **The first premise says nothing about what happens when someone does not study**, so **not studying does not settle whether the grade was good.** **A person could get a good grade for some other reason.**"]}, {"id": 450, "a": ["**Across the four types, the two valid syllogisms were judged correctly at 97% and 60%, while the two invalid ones were judged correctly at 40% each.**", "- **Both valid types were judged correctly more often than both invalid types.**"]}, {"id": 451, "a": ["The **Wason four-card problem** **tests the effect of using real-world items in a conditional-reasoning problem.**", "- **Four cards read E, K, 4 and 7.**", "- **The task is to determine the minimum number of cards to turn over to test the rule if there is a vowel on one side, then there is an even number on the other side.**"]}, {"id": 452, "a": ["**The answer is to turn over the E and the 7.**", "- **E needs to be turned over.** **46% of participants indicated that 4 would need to be turned over, which is an error.**", "- **Only 7% indicated that 7 needs to be turned over, which is correct.**"]}, {"id": 453, "a": ["**A 7 with a vowel on its other side would break the rule**, so **turning over the 7 is a real test of it.**", "- **A 4 cannot break the rule whatever is on its back**, so **turning it over does not test the rule.**"]}, {"id": 454, "a": ["The **falsification principle** states that **to test a rule, you must look for situations that falsify the rule.**", "- **To falsify a rule is to prove that it is false.**", "- **Most participants fail to do this, which is why so few chose the 7 card.**"]}, {"id": 455, "a": ["**When the problem is stated in concrete everyday terms, correct responses greatly increase.** **In the concrete version, the rule is If drinking beer, then 21 years old, and the four cards read Beer, Soda, 16 years old and 21 years old.**", "- **73% of participants answered this version correctly.**", "- **Following the falsification principle, the cards to turn over are beer and 16 years old**, because **someone drinking beer while under 21 is the only case that breaks the rule.**"]}, {"id": 456, "a": ["A **permission schema** is **the idea that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out.**", "- **A schema is a familiar pattern of knowledge**, and **a permission schema is the pattern of a rule that grants permission once a condition is met.**"]}, {"id": 457, "a": ["**Permission schemas are used in the concrete versions, since people are familiar with rules of this kind, such as a drinking age.**", "- **In the beer version, being 21 years old is the condition that must be satisfied, and drinking beer is what can then be carried out.**", "- **Context is important, because the same logical problem becomes easier in a familiar setting that calls up a permission schema.** **Familiarity is not always important, so the improvement does not come from familiarity alone.**"]}, {"id": 458, "a": ["In **Kermer and colleagues (2006)**, **people expected a loss to hurt about twice as much as a gain helps**, but **the actual drop was only about 1 point.**", "- **Risk aversion partly rests on overestimated negative feelings**, since expected emotions are one determinant of it."]}, {"id": 459, "a": ["**Expected emotions** are **emotions that people predict they will feel concerning an outcome**, so they come from the decision itself: the person imagines how a good or bad result would feel.", "- **Incidental emotions are not specifically related to decision making.** They come from **general disposition, a recent experience or the surroundings**, such as **weather** or **mood from an unrelated film clip.**", "- Expected emotions are one determinant of risk aversion, while incidental emotions show how feelings that have nothing to do with the choice can still change it."]}, {"id": 460, "a": ["In **Simonsohn (2007)**, applicants’ academic attributes were **weighted more heavily on cloudy days than on sunny days**, and in **Simonsohn (2009)**, visitors to a highly rated university were **more likely to enroll if they visited on a cloudy day.** In **Lerner and colleagues (2004)**, mood from film clips changed buying and selling prices.", "- In none of these was the emotion related to the choice: **the weather has nothing to do with the applicants or the quality of the school**, and film clips have nothing to do with highlighters.", "- These are **incidental emotions**, and yet they changed the decisions."]}, {"id": 461, "a": ["**The same cesarean test case drew different recommendations after different earlier cases.**", "- **The same choice drew over 99% versus below 28% consent depending on opt-out or opt-in.**", "- In both, **decisions depend on more than the options themselves.**"]}, {"id": 462, "a": ["**Many people do nothing either way**, so **the default sets what happens if they do nothing.**", "- Under **opt-in**, doing nothing leaves a person a **non-donor**, and under **opt-out**, a **donor.**"]}, {"id": 463, "a": ["In **Tversky and Kahneman (1981)**, **Program A and C have the same outcome and B and D the same gamble.**", "- **72 percent chose the sure option in the gain frame, and 78 percent chose the gamble in the loss frame.**", "- **Only the wording changed**, which is the framing effect."]}, {"id": 464, "a": ["**Expected emotions** involve losses and gains this way: **people expect a loss to have a greater impact than a gain of the same size**, which **increases risk aversion.**", "- In the **framing effect of Tversky and Kahneman (1981)**, **gain wording leads to the sure option**, a **risk-aversion strategy**, and **loss wording leads to the gamble**, a **risk-taking strategy** that accepts uncertainty in the hope of avoiding a certain loss.", "- Both involve how gains and losses are perceived, and in both the decision follows the perceived impact and not only the outcome."]}, {"id": 465, "a": ["**Utility theory says to accept any offer.**", "- In **Sanfey and colleagues (2003)**, **participants rejected low offers when angered by unfairness, giving up money.**"]}, {"id": 466, "a": ["In **Sanfey and colleagues (2003)**, **anger at unfair offers explains why they were rejected more often from humans than from computers**: participants were angrier at unfairness from a person.", "- **Separately, more right anterior insula activation, an area connected with emotional states, went with rejecting more offers.**", "- Rejecting means getting nothing, so the emotion linked to this area led people to give up money, which shows that **emotion is important in decision making.**"]}, {"id": 467, "a": ["In the **ultimatum game of Sanfey and colleagues (2003)**, **emotion linked to right anterior insula activation, together with anger at unfair offers, led participants to give up money by rejecting them.** The feeling about an offer shaped the choice about money.", "- In **Kermer and colleagues (2006)**, participants **expected losing $3 to hurt about twice as much as winning $5 would help**, but **the actual drop from losing was only about 1 point**, so they **overestimated the negative feeling.**", "- In both, emotions, felt in the moment or predicted in advance, are part of choices about money."]}, {"id": 468, "a": ["A syllogism is **valid if its conclusion follows logically from its premises**, and **if the premises of a valid syllogism are true, the conclusion must be true.** Validity is about the form of the reasoning, and truth is about whether statements match the world.", "- So **a valid form with a false premise can still produce a false conclusion**, as in the birds and four legs example, and **a syllogism can be invalid even though every statement seems reasonable.**", "- **Validity therefore does not guarantee a true conclusion, and believability does not guarantee validity.**"]}, {"id": 469, "a": ["**Belief bias** is **the tendency to think that a syllogism is valid if its conclusion is believable**, so believability interferes with judging validity.", "- **71% of participants judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid.**", "- This happens because **good reasoning and truth are not the same thing**: a conclusion can be believable and still not follow from the premises."]}, {"id": 470, "a": ["In the **mental model approach (Johnson-Laird, 1999)**, a person **creates a model of the situation described by the premises** and then **generates tentative conclusions** that might hold. The key step is **looking for exceptions to falsify the model**: a case in which the premises are true but the conclusion is not.", "- **A conclusion is valid only if it cannot be refuted by any model of the premises.** To refute a conclusion is to prove that it can be false.", "- So **finding even one exception matters**, because **if even one model fits the premises but not the conclusion, the conclusion does not follow.**"]}, {"id": 471, "a": ["In the **mental model approach**, the person **looks for exceptions to falsify the model**, searching for a case where the premises are true but the conclusion is not. In the **Wason four-card problem**, the **falsification principle** says **to test a rule, you must look for situations that falsify the rule.**", "- Both treat good reasoning as **looking for a case that could show the conclusion or rule to be false**, and not as piling up cases that fit.", "- In the card problem, that is why **the 7 is a real test of the rule while the 4 cannot break it**, and in a syllogism it is why one counterexample model rejects a conclusion."]}, {"id": 472, "a": ["**Modus ponens** and **modus tollens** are both **valid**, because each follows from **If p, then q** together with its second premise. **Modus ponens** goes from **p** to **therefore q**, and **modus tollens** goes from **not q** to **therefore not p.**", "- **97% judged modus ponens correctly and only 60% judged modus tollens correctly.**", "- **Modus tollens reasons from a result that did not happen back to a condition that did not**: if studying always leads to a good grade, then not getting the grade means the studying did not happen. It is **just as valid as modus ponens, yet people find it much harder to evaluate.**"]}, {"id": 473, "a": ["**Conditional syllogisms 3 and 4 are not valid**, and they share one problem. **Syllogism 3** goes from **q** to **therefore p**, and **syllogism 4** goes from **not p** to **therefore not q.**", "- In both cases the conclusion treats the condition as the only route to the result. **The first premise says what happens when someone studies, but it does not say that studying is the only way to get a good grade**, and it **says nothing about what happens when someone does not study.**", "- **Only 40% judged each of them correctly.** The Tucson and Arizona example shows it: living in Arizona does not mean living in Tucson."]}, {"id": 474, "a": ["In the abstract **Wason four-card problem**, the rule is **if there is a vowel on one side, then there is an even number on the other.** **The 7 is the falsifying card**: **a 7 with a vowel on its other side would break the rule.** A **4 cannot break the rule whatever is on its back.**", "- **Only 7% chose the 7, and 46% chose the 4**, which reflects that **most participants fail to look for situations that falsify the rule**, the thing the **falsification principle** requires.", "- **With the concrete beer version, 73% answered correctly**, because **a familiar permission rule such as a drinking age makes the falsifying cards clearer**: beer and 16 years old."]}, {"id": 475, "a": ["A **permission schema** is **the idea that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out**, a familiar **pattern of knowledge** about rules that grant permission once a condition is met.", "- In the **beer version**, **being 21 years old is the condition that must be satisfied, and drinking beer is what can then be carried out.** People are familiar with this kind of rule, such as a drinking age.", "- **Context is important**, because the same logical problem becomes easier in a familiar setting that calls up a permission schema, although **familiarity alone does not explain the improvement.**"]}, {"id": 476, "a": ["A **categorical syllogism** **describes the relation between two categories using all, no or some.** An example is **All birds are animals, All animals eat food, therefore all birds eat food.**", "- A **conditional syllogism** has **a first premise of the form If p, then q**, where **p stands for a condition and q for what follows when that condition is met.**", "- Its **four types differ in the second premise, which states p, not p, q or not q**, and in the conclusion drawn from it. **Two of the four are valid and two are not.**"]}, {"id": 477, "a": ["**Emotions such as anger, anxiety or sadness change choices.** **Anger at unfair offers led participants to reject money**, **anxious people avoid decisions that could lead to large negative consequences**, and **sad mood changed buying and selling prices.**", "- **Reasoning biases such as belief bias reduce logical judgments**: **71% judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid.**", "- Neither emotion nor biased reasoning follows a purely rational standard, such as accepting any offer in the ultimatum game or judging a syllogism only by its form."]}, {"id": 478, "a": ["The **phonological similarity effect** is the finding that **lists of similar-sounding items are recalled worse than lists of different-sounding items**, and this holds **even when the items are only read silently**. Nothing has to be spoken aloud for the cost to appear.", "- **Acoustic similarity effect is arguably the better name**, because the confusion is about how the items sound and not about how they look.", "- The effect matters because it shows what kind of code is being used in short-term memory, which is the point developed in the next questions."]}, {"id": 479, "a": ["The effect shows a speech-based code because **people recode what they see into a speech-based form.** If memory held only the visual appearance of the items, the mix-ups would be between **look-alikes such as E and F**, and instead the mix-ups follow sound.", "- Letters that look different but sound alike are the ones that get confused, which is what a **purely visual code would not predict.**", "- This is **the basis of the phonological loop in working memory**, the component that keeps speech-based information available."]}, {"id": 480, "a": ["The **phonological loop** has two parts: a **phonological store** and an **articulatory control process.** The control process **converts visual input to speech and refreshes the traces** in the store, which is how seen items come to be held as sounds.", "- **Articulatory suppression occupies that process.** When the process is occupied, it is not available to convert and refresh the items.", "- As a result, **with visually presented lists the similarity cost disappears**, which shows that the cost depended on that conversion into speech."]}, {"id": 481, "a": ["A word’s **neighborhood** is **the set of words that differ from it by one letter** (an **orthographic** neighborhood) **or one sound** (a **phonological** neighborhood).", "- **Cat** is a **large neighborhood** word, because many words differ from it by a single letter or sound.", "- **Hippopotamus** is a **small neighborhood** word, because very few words are that close to it."]}, {"id": 482, "a": ["**Roodenrys et al. (2002)** found that **large neighborhood words are recalled better in short-term memory tasks.**", "- Words with many close neighbors are easier to recall than words with few.", "- The finding means that **long-term memory knowledge of words helps short-term recall**, so what a person already knows about words contributes to what they can hold temporarily."]}, {"id": 483, "a": ["**Memory span is not a pure measure of short-term capacity.** The neighborhood size effect shows that **long-term memory knowledge of words helps short-term recall**, so a span score reflects more than how much short-term memory can hold.", "- Two people, or two lists, with the same short-term capacity could still produce different spans if the words differ in neighborhood size.", "- Span therefore mixes short-term capacity with the contribution of long-term word knowledge."]}, {"id": 484, "a": ["In the **Brown–Peterson task**, **people study consonants and then do a distractor task, counting backwards, that blocks rehearsal.** The distractor takes the place of the repetition that would normally keep the letters in mind.", "- **The longer the distraction, the worse the recall.**", "- The task is designed to show what happens to information in short-term memory when it cannot be rehearsed."]}, {"id": 485, "a": ["**Peterson and Peterson (1959)** reasoned about why recall got worse. **Because counting is so unlike remembering letters, they judged interference an unlikely cause**, since the distractor did not resemble the material to be remembered.", "- They **concluded that short-term memory (STM) loses information through decay unless it is rehearsed.**", "- In their view, the information fades with time when nothing keeps it active."]}, {"id": 486, "a": ["The conclusion of **Peterson and Peterson (1959)** **challenged the view, following McGeoch’s (1932) critique, that forgetting is due to interference.**", "- **Decay is the researchers’ conclusion**: they argued that forgetting in short-term memory comes from information fading unless it is rehearsed.", "- **Keppel and Underwood (1962) are cited for an alternative**, so there is a competing account of the same forgetting."]}, {"id": 487, "a": ["The **modality effect** is **better memory for the last one or two items of a list when it is heard than when it is read silently.**", "- **Lip-read or silently mouthed lists show it too**, so the advantage is not limited to lists that are actually heard.", "- It concerns the end of the list specifically, and the way the list is presented changes how well those final items are remembered."]}, {"id": 488, "a": ["The modality effect **strongly influenced ideas about sensory memory**, which is **a back-up store of raw, unanalyzed sensory input.**", "- The better recall of the last items for heard lists was taken as evidence that some raw sensory information remains briefly available.", "- Sensory memory is therefore described as a store that holds input before it has been analyzed."]}, {"id": 489, "a": ["**Neath and Surprenant (2003)** favor an explanation based on what hearing adds: **hearing adds extra information about how the list sounds, which helps the final items.**", "- On this account the advantage does not require a separate raw sensory store, because the extra sound information is what helps the last items.", "- It is described as the favored explanation of the modality effect."]}, {"id": 490, "a": ["**Early work suggested people can report only a small part of a brief display**, which seemed to say that perception holds very little. **Sperling (1960)** argued that **this limit comes from recall, not perception.**", "- The limit lies in reporting what was seen, and not in how much was registered.", "- That reasoning is what led to the partial report method described next."]}, {"id": 491, "a": ["In **Sperling (1960)**, with **partial report, a cue right after the display lets observers report the cued part almost perfectly.**", "- Because the observer did not know which part would be cued, being able to report any cued part means **a sensory (iconic) store briefly held nearly all of the display.**", "- Partial report therefore shows that far more was registered than the limited report suggested."]}, {"id": 492, "a": ["In **Sperling’s (1960)** work, **the real limit is the time needed to report.**", "- **If the cue is delayed, performance falls toward the usual limit**, which is what happens when the observer has to report a large part of the display.", "- The decline happens **because the store is very short-lived**, so the information fades before it can all be reported."]}, {"id": 493, "a": ["The **position error effect** is that **items are usually recalled in the correct position**, and **when they are not, the errors are systematic.** The misplaced item lands **in an adjacent position.**", "- An item recalled in the wrong place is therefore usually off by only one position, and not placed anywhere at random.", "- This shows that order information is held with reasonable accuracy even when recall is not perfect."]}, {"id": 494, "a": ["**Order errors are not random.** They follow a pattern, with misplaced items falling next to their correct positions.", "- **Scoring only percent correct hides that people keep approximate position information**, because a nearly correct placement counts the same as a wildly wrong one.", "- Looking at the type of error therefore reveals what memory retains that a simple score does not."]}, {"id": 495, "a": ["**People seem to use mental images, but self-reports of imagery are unreliable**, because **it is impossible to tell whether the images differ or only the descriptions.** Two people might describe imagery differently while having the same images, or similar descriptions of different images.", "- **Researchers therefore use tasks that require imagery and vary a critical part of the task**, so that the effect of imagery can be measured in performance.", "- The mental rotation task is the example that follows."]}, {"id": 496, "a": ["**Shepard and Metzler (1971)** gave people a task that required imagery: they **judged whether two block shapes were the same or mirror images, with one turned.**", "- To decide, people had to compare the shapes even though one was turned away from the other.", "- The critical part of the task that was varied was **how far one shape was turned**, which is what allowed imagery to be studied."]}, {"id": 497, "a": ["In **Shepard and Metzler (1971)**, **people mentally rotated one shape**, and **the larger the turn, the longer the response, at a constant rate.**", "- The steady increase in response time with the size of the turn fits the idea that the person turns an image through the angle, much as a real object would be turned.", "- This suggests **mental images are much like real images**, and it **influenced theories of mental representation.**"]}, {"id": 498, "a": ["**People judge probability with heuristics that trade accuracy for speed.** They are **usually good enough, but sometimes wrong.**", "- **Tversky and Kahneman (1983)** showed that **estimates often differ greatly from objective probability.**", "- A shortcut is quick and usually works, but when it fails the judged probability can be far from the real probability."]}, {"id": 499, "a": ["The **conjunction fallacy** is **judging that A and B together are more likely than A alone or B alone.**", "- This is an error because **a combination is never more likely than either part alone**: every case of A and B is also a case of A, and also a case of B.", "- It is a mistake of probability that heuristics can produce."]}, {"id": 500, "a": ["**Shafir, Smith, and Osherson (1990)** found that **people sometimes rated a combination of professions or activities as more likely than one part.**", "- That is the conjunction fallacy in action: a combination judged more probable than one of its parts.", "- The finding **points to heuristics rather than probability rules** as the basis of such judgments."]}, {"id": 501, "a": ["In the **Wason Selection Task**, the rule is **If a card has a D on one side, it has a 3 on the other.** The rule is **best tested with D and 7**, **since the 7 could falsify it.** A 7 with a D on the other side would break the rule.", "- **Most people choose D and 3.** The D is a good choice, but **the 3 can only confirm the rule**, since a 3 with any letter on the back does not contradict it.", "- Choosing a card that can only confirm tests the rule less well than choosing a card that could show it to be false."]}, {"id": 502, "a": ["**The same logic in a familiar thematic rule is much easier.** With the rule **If you borrow my car, you must fill up the gas tank**, the cards to check are **borrowed car** and **empty gas tank.**", "- Someone who borrowed the car and left it empty is the case that breaks the rule, so those are the cards that could falsify it.", "- The structure is the same as the D and 3 rule, yet people handle it far better when the content is familiar."]}, {"id": 503, "a": ["**Wason and Shapiro (1971)** compared **abstract and thematic rules** in the selection task.", "- **People test a rule well with relevant experience**, as in the thematic rule about borrowing a car.", "- **Abstract rules force them to use logic alone**, and without the support of experience performance is worse."]}, {"id": 504, "a": ["**Framing effects occur when the wording or context makes a big difference to a choice, even for otherwise equivalent situations.**", "- **Kahneman and Tversky (1982)** summarized many of them.", "- The key point is that the situations are equivalent in substance, yet different wording or context leads to different choices."]}, {"id": 505, "a": ["In classic framing research (**Tversky and Kahneman**), **the same choice can be presented in two ways.** **Gain wording typically leads to the sure option and loss wording to the gamble.**", "- The outcomes are the same in both versions, and only the description changes.", "- **Choices can therefore follow the framing and not the substance.**"]}, {"id": 506, "a": ["The **Monty Hall 3 Door problem** shows **poor intuition about the probability of combinations of events.**", "- **After you pick one of three doors, the host, who always plays fair this way, opens a no-prize door.**", "- The decision is then whether to stay with the original door or switch to the remaining unopened door."]}, {"id": 507, "a": ["**You should switch**, which **wins about two-thirds of the time.** **Your first pick keeps its 1 in 3 chance**, because the host opening a no-prize door does not change the chance that the first pick was right.", "- **The 2 in 3 held by the other two doors moves to the one remaining door**, which is why the remaining door has the better chance.", "- **Many people think stay or switch makes no difference**, which is incorrect."]}, {"id": 508, "a": ["The Monty Hall problem shows that **intuition about the probability of combinations of events is poor.**", "- The common feeling that two doors left means a one-in-two chance for each is wrong.", "- The correct reasoning keeps the original 1 in 3 chance for the first pick, and so favors switching."]}, {"id": 509, "a": ["**Risky choices depend on how outcomes are framed and perceived.** In these choices **people pick between a guaranteed outcome and a gamble**, and the **guaranteed outcome can be a gain or a loss.**", "- The same structure of choice can therefore be felt very differently depending on whether the sure outcome is something gained or something lost."]}, {"id": 510, "a": ["In **prospect-theory research (Kahneman and Tversky)**, **people typically avoid risk with gains, taking the sure thing, and seek risk with losses, taking the gamble.**", "- With a possible gain, the guaranteed amount is preferred to a chance of more.", "- With a possible loss, people prefer to gamble over accepting the guaranteed loss."]}, {"id": 511, "a": ["**Decisions about risk depend on whether outcomes are seen as gains or losses, not only on the objective odds.**", "- If only the odds mattered, the same choice would be treated the same way whatever it was called.", "- The pattern of avoiding risk for gains and seeking it for losses shows that the **perception of the outcome** matters as well."]}, {"id": 512, "a": ["**German and Defeyter (2000)** tested **whether young children get stuck on an object’s usual function, which is functional fixedness.**", "- They used **a box that was first shown as a container.**", "- The question was whether having seen the box used as a container would make it harder to use it for another purpose, such as a support."]}, {"id": 513, "a": ["In **German and Defeyter (2000)**, **older children (6 and 7) were slower to use the box as a support after seeing it as a container.** Seeing the usual function made them slower to use the object differently.", "- **5-year-olds were not slowed and were even faster.**", "- The difference was between age groups, with the younger children showing no slowing."]}, {"id": 514, "a": ["**Younger children seem to have a more fluid idea of function**, so **one demonstrated use did not lock the object in.**", "- The 5-year-olds’ results suggest they were not fixed on the box’s container function, so they could use it as a support readily.", "- Functional fixedness, in this account, develops with age."]}, {"id": 515, "a": ["**Leist and colleagues (2022)** studied the **irrelevant speech effect**: **speech to be ignored impairs serial recall.**", "- The impairment is **greater for changing-state speech than for steady-state speech.**", "- The speech is irrelevant to the task, yet it still disrupts recall of a list in order."]}, {"id": 516, "a": ["**Leist and colleagues (2022)** found that **speech hurt verbal recall but not spatial recall**, in both children and adults.", "- Irrelevant speech disrupted memory for verbal items, and it did not disrupt memory for spatial items.", "- The same pattern held across age groups."]}, {"id": 517, "a": ["The result of **Leist and colleagues (2022)** shows that **the effect is specific to verbal material.**", "- That **fits phonological interference better than attention-capture or changing-state accounts.**", "- The specific effect on verbal recall is what points to interference within the phonological system."]}, {"id": 518, "a": ["**Morrison and Richmond (2020)** studied **cognitive offloading**, which is **using actions like writing things down to reduce mental demands**, as described by **Risko and Gilbert (2016).**", "- Offloading moves some of the work of remembering from the mind to an action in the world.", "- The study asked how offloading relates to memory load and to memory ability."]}, {"id": 519, "a": ["In **Morrison and Richmond (2020)**, **offloading improved memory, especially at high loads.**", "- **People offloaded more as load grew**, so they used the strategy more when the memory demand was greater.", "- Offloading is therefore most helpful, and most used, when the memory task is hardest."]}, {"id": 520, "a": ["No. In **Morrison and Richmond (2020)**, **memory ability did not predict who offloaded.**", "- People with better and worse memory ability were not distinguished by how much they offloaded.", "- **Offloading may therefore be a compensatory strategy across abilities**, used by people at different ability levels."]}, {"id": 521, "a": ["The **bat-and-ball problem (Frederick, 2005)** states that **a bat and ball cost $1.10 and the bat costs $1.00 more.**", "- **The intuitive answer is 10 cents; the correct answer is 5 cents.**", "- The problem is used because the quick answer that comes to mind is wrong."]}, {"id": 522, "a": ["**Kahneman and Frederick (2002)** called the error **unconscious attribute substitution.**", "- **De Neys and colleagues (2013)** found that **wrong reasoners were less confident than on a control version.**", "- Their lower confidence suggests that people who gave the wrong answer showed some sensitivity to the problem."]}, {"id": 523, "a": ["**Hoover and Healy (2019)** found that **wrong reasoners mostly remembered the phrase more than** from the problem.", "- **Because substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause of the error, they call it a sensitivity error.**", "- Their finding changes how the bat-and-ball error is explained, since it does not rest on substitution being shown to occur."]}, {"id": 524, "a": ["**Giuliani and colleagues (2023)** combined **the framing effect** with **the default effect.**", "- The **framing effect** is **a sure option for gains and a risky option for losses.**", "- The **default effect** is **choosing the preselected option**, so the study asked how the two work together."]}, {"id": 525, "a": ["In **Giuliani and colleagues (2023)**, **a risky default raised risk taking**, but **a sure default did not raise risk aversion.**", "- **The two effects were additive**, so the influence of the frame and the influence of the default added together.", "- The result is a combined picture of how wording and preselected options shape risky choice."]}, {"id": 526, "a": ["In **Giuliani and colleagues (2023)**, there was **an exploratory question asked after the choice.**", "- **The belief about the default’s origin went with following or rejecting it.**", "- What people believed about where the default came from was therefore related to whether they followed or rejected it."]}, {"id": 527, "a": ["**Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013)** argue that **adolescents reason about risk like adults in the lab.**", "- **The peer context is what raises risk taking.**", "- The risk is therefore not due to poorer reasoning on its own but to the presence of peers."]}, {"id": 528, "a": ["**Chein and colleagues (2011)** scanned people during **a driving game played alone or watched by friends.**", "- **Adolescents took more risks when watched**, and they showed **greater reward-region activity.**", "- The pattern ties peer presence to both more risky behavior and more activity in the brain’s reward regions."]}, {"id": 529, "a": ["No. In **Chein and colleagues (2011)**, **cognitive-control regions did not change with peers.**", "- Because control regions stayed the same and reward-region activity rose, **the authors concluded the effect worked mainly through reward circuitry.**", "- Peers affected the reward system more than the control system."]}, {"id": 530, "a": ["The **phonological similarity effect** shows **a speech-based code in the phonological loop**, while the **Brown–Peterson task** asks **how short-term memory loses information when rehearsal is blocked.**", "- Both concern short-term memory, but they address different questions: **how information is coded**, and **why information is lost.**", "- The similarity effect is about the form of the code, and the Brown–Peterson task is about forgetting."]}, {"id": 531, "a": ["**Peterson and Peterson (1959)** judged **interference an unlikely cause** because **counting is so unlike remembering letters**, and **concluded that short-term memory loses information through decay unless it is rehearsed.**", "- **Decay** is loss through the passage of time, and **interference** is loss from competing information, so the two are different explanations of the same forgetting.", "- The conclusion **challenged the view, following McGeoch’s (1932) critique, that forgetting is due to interference**, and **Keppel and Underwood (1962) are cited for an alternative.**"]}, {"id": 532, "a": ["The **neighborhood size effect of Roodenrys et al. (2002)** shows that **long-term word knowledge helps short-term recall**, so **span is not a pure measure of short-term capacity.**", "- The **similarity effect** shows **the speech-based code used in short-term memory.**", "- One shows a contribution from long-term memory, and the other shows how the information is coded in short-term memory."]}, {"id": 533, "a": ["The **modality effect** **influenced ideas about a raw sensory back-up store.**", "- **Sperling’s (1960) partial report** showed that **an iconic store briefly holds nearly all of a display.**", "- Both concern the **short-lived store of sensory information**, one through memory for lists and the other through memory for visual displays."]}, {"id": 534, "a": ["**Percent correct treats a misplaced item as simply wrong.**", "- The **position error effect** shows that **errors land in adjacent positions**, so **people keep approximate position information.**", "- Percent correct hides this information, while looking at the pattern of errors reveals it."]}, {"id": 535, "a": ["In **Shepard and Metzler (1971)**, **response time rose at a constant rate with the angle of turn.**", "- That is a measurable pattern in behavior, which means **imagery can be studied without relying on self-reports.**", "- The pattern supports **image-like representations**, while avoiding the unreliable descriptions that people give of their images."]}, {"id": 536, "a": ["**Heuristics trade accuracy for speed**, the **conjunction fallacy** is **judging a combination as more likely than a part**, and **framing** lets **wording change a choice.**", "- In each case, the judgment follows a shortcut or the wording and not the objective probability or the substance of the situation.", "- They share the idea that quick or surface-driven judgments can depart from what is actually the case."]}, {"id": 537, "a": ["In the **Wason Selection Task**, people **choose D and 3**, and the **3 can only confirm the rule.**", "- In the **Monty Hall problem**, **many people think stay or switch makes no difference**, but **switching wins about two-thirds of the time.**", "- Each shows **poor intuition** about what the evidence implies: a card that cannot test the rule, and a probability that does not split evenly."]}, {"id": 538, "a": ["**Wason and Shapiro (1971)** compared abstract and thematic rules and found that **with relevant experience people test a rule well**, while **abstract rules force them to use logic alone.**", "- A thematic rule such as borrowing a car ties the cards to situations people know, so the cards that could break the rule are easier to see.", "- With an abstract rule about D and 3, there is no such support, and people choose cards that can only confirm."]}, {"id": 539, "a": ["Both framing and risky decisions show **choices depending on whether outcomes are seen as gains or losses.**", "- **The sure option is favored for gains, and the gamble for losses.**", "- Framing is the general finding that wording changes choices, and risky decisions are the case where the choice is between a guaranteed outcome and a gamble."]}, {"id": 540, "a": ["**Giuliani and colleagues (2023)** combined **framing with defaults.**", "- The classic framing result is a sure option for gains and a risky option for losses, and they added the default effect, choosing the preselected option.", "- **A risky default raised risk taking, while the two effects were additive**, so the default added its own influence to the framing influence."]}, {"id": 541, "a": ["**Functional fixedness** is **getting stuck on an object’s usual function.**", "- In **German and Defeyter (2000)**, **younger children showed immunity to it**: **one demonstrated use did not lock the object in.**", "- The result relates functional fixedness to age, with older children but not 5-year-olds slowed after seeing the box as a container."]}, {"id": 542, "a": ["**Leist and colleagues (2022)** found that **irrelevant speech hurt verbal but not spatial recall**, and the **phonological similarity effect** is that **similar-sounding items are recalled worse.**", "- Both depend on **speech-based, phonological processes.**", "- In each, the difficulty affects verbal material specifically, whether through similar sounds or through speech to be ignored."]}, {"id": 543, "a": ["In **Morrison and Richmond (2020)**, **offloading helped most at high loads and increased as load grew.**", "- **Ability did not predict who did it.**", "- Offloading therefore relates to the memory load the person faces, and not to how good their memory is, which fits **a compensatory strategy.**"]}, {"id": 544, "a": ["**Heuristics trade accuracy for speed and are usually good enough but sometimes wrong.**", "- **In the bat-and-ball problem, the intuitive answer is 10 cents and the correct answer is 5 cents.**", "- Both say that an answer that feels natural can be wrong."]}, {"id": 545, "a": ["**Hoover and Healy (2019)** change the explanation of the error. They found that **wrong reasoners mostly remembered the phrase more than.**", "- **They say attribute substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause of the error**, so **they call it a sensitivity error.**", "- The earlier explanation by **Kahneman and Frederick (2002)** was unconscious attribute substitution."]}, {"id": 546, "a": ["**Adolescents reason like adults in the lab**, according to **Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013)**, but **when watched by friends they take more risks with greater reward-region activity**, as found by **Chein and colleagues (2011).**", "- **The authors concluded the effect worked mainly through reward circuitry and not changed cognitive control.**", "- Together they locate the effect of peers in the reward system."]}, {"id": 547, "a": ["**A risky default raised risk taking** in **Giuliani and colleagues (2023)**, and **peers raised adolescent risk taking** in **Chein and colleagues (2011).**", "- **Context and not only reasoning changes risk.**", "- In each case something about the situation, a preselected option or the presence of friends, shifted risky choice."]}, {"id": 548, "a": ["In the **conjunction fallacy**, **people judge A and B together as more likely than A alone**, although **a combination is never more likely than either part.**", "- In the **Wason Selection Task**, **most people choose D and 3, and the 3 can only confirm the rule, while D and 7 test it.**", "- Each shows people departing from a rule of probability or logic: one ignores that a combination cannot be more likely than a part, and the other fails to look for a card that could falsify the rule."]}, {"id": 549, "a": ["In the **Brown–Peterson task**, **a counting distractor blocks rehearsal and longer distraction gave worse recall.**", "- In **Sperling’s (1960) partial report**, **delaying the cue made performance fall toward the usual limit**, because the store is very short-lived.", "- Both use a manipulation of time or distraction to study how long information lasts: one in short-term memory with rehearsal blocked, and one in a brief sensory store."]}];
